{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Edqd4LLw6tsb7q8JKTPbUy","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (85.9KB). Full output saved to: /home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-012138--x6981e3-work/d022da92-c114-4f47-a8f5-ca68c59e3f52/tool-results/b7q8ktfej.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\ntotal 104\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 micha micha  4096 Nov  5 01:21 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 micha micha  4096 Nov  5 01:21 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 75100 Nov  5 01:21 PLAN.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha   219 Nov  5 01:21 STAGE.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 12610 Nov  5 01:21 TASK.md\n----\n# Game plan: an original fitness comedy inspired by Wii Fit\n\nYou are the builder. Build this game from this plan and your brief. Nobody will answer questions, so where the plan leaves something open, decide and move on.\n\n- **The title is yours.** Pick it the way your brief says. It lives in one constant (`src/data/title.js`) and nothing else hard-codes it. In this plan `{TITLE}` stands for it.\n- **Paths are relative to your dev folder.** \"Root\" means its parent, where your brief puts the deliverable html and the exit-interview file. Use the names your brief gives.\n- **✔ means the director ran it** on this machine against engine kit 0.46.0 and hub client 1.4.0, in the form written here. Don't re-derive it. If your download is newer, read its changelog from 0.46.0 on.\n- **Read in this order:** this plan; the kit's `GUIDE.md` and `ASSETS.md`, whole; the hub's `README.md`, whole. Search `API.md` and `LIBRARY.md`; don't read them whole.\n- Your brief's rules on folders, ports, processes, browsers and test reporting still apply. They are repeated here only where they shape the work.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**A week at a spotless wellness island run by a small, polite, deeply judgmental bathroom scale. Your whole body is one dot. Keep it in the circle.**\n\n- **Genre:** fitness and balance minigame collection, inside a light life-sim week. (Catalogue genre: Sports.)\n- **Perspective:** isometric on the island; fixed front-on for class activities; other fixed views per activity (section 5).\n- **Length:** a story week of 7 short days, 16 minutes at the quickest and about an hour for a player who tries everything. Everything is also playable at once from Drop-In Class.\n\n### Pillars (use these to settle any open question)\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d022da92-c114-4f47-a8f5-ca68c59e3f52","uuid":"bc829727-cfb5-4f23-ac9c-4e82d5f7a1be","timestamp":"2026-11-05T08:21:44.631Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 104\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 micha micha  4096 Nov  5 01:21 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 micha micha  4096 Nov  5 01:21 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 75100 Nov  5 01:21 PLAN.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha   219 Nov  5 01:21 STAGE.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 12610 Nov  5 01:21 TASK.md\n----\n# Game plan: an original fitness comedy inspired by Wii Fit\n\nYou are the builder. Build this game from this plan and your brief. Nobody will answer questions, so where the plan leaves something open, decide and move on.\n\n- **The title is yours.** Pick it the way your brief says. It lives in one constant (`src/data/title.js`) and nothing else hard-codes it. In this plan `{TITLE}` stands for it.\n- **Paths are relative to your dev folder.** \"Root\" means its parent, where your brief puts the deliverable html and the exit-interview file. Use the names your brief gives.\n- **✔ means the director ran it** on this machine against engine kit 0.46.0 and hub client 1.4.0, in the form written here. Don't re-derive it. If your download is newer, read its changelog from 0.46.0 on.\n- **Read in this order:** this plan; the kit's `GUIDE.md` and `ASSETS.md`, whole; the hub's `README.md`, whole. Search `API.md` and `LIBRARY.md`; don't read them whole.\n- Your brief's rules on folders, ports, processes, browsers and test reporting still apply. They are repeated here only where they shape the work.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**A week at a spotless wellness island run by a small, polite, deeply judgmental bathroom scale. Your whole body is one dot. Keep it in the circle.**\n\n- **Genre:** fitness and balance minigame collection, inside a light life-sim week. (Catalogue genre: Sports.)\n- **Perspective:** isometric on the island; fixed front-on for class activities; other fixed views per activity (section 5).\n- **Length:** a story week of 7 short days, 16 minutes at the quickest and about an hour for a player who tries everything. Everything is also playable at once from Drop-In Class.\n\n### Pillars (use these to settle any open question)\n\n1. **You are a dot.** Almost everything is one verb: lean. The left stick, WASD or the mouse moves your centre of balance, and it has weight and sway. Poses, reps, hoops, slacklines and the finale are all different ways of moving or holding the Dot.\n2. **Politely judged.** Everything you do gets a calm, clinical, faintly wounding remark. You cannot lose. You can only be noted.\n3. **One small day at a time.** Short sessions, a morning number, a chart, a jar slowly filling.\n4. **Always in class.** Other people are always beside you: classmates alone, family on the couch, friends as ghosts online. Everyone wobbles.\n\n### What it takes from the source, and how it stays original\n\n| Distinctive in the source | Here |\n|---|---|\n| Your body is the controller; a centre-of-balance dot | The Dot: real sway physics on a stick, keys or mouse |\n| A talking device that politely judges you | Ounce, a mint retro dial scale whose dial is its face |\n| A calm instructor demonstrating | Coach Plumb, 81, unblinking, never off vertical |\n| Daily check-in, one number that sums you up, a graph | Weigh-In, Wobble Age, a line chart with a twist |\n| Yoga, strength, aerobics, balance games | Four stations on the island |\n| Minutes banked toward unlocks | Drips in the Sweat Jar |\n| A bright, clean, soft-spoken world | White tile plaza on a pastel island made of giant gym equipment |\n| A household sharing one device | Family Hour on one TV, AI Hub sessions online |\n\n**Do not use** any Nintendo name, character, look, music, level or minigame name: no Wii, Mii, Balance Board, Wii Fit Trainer, Wuhu Island or Fit Age. No white-board host, no sound-alike tunes, no channel-style menus. Mechanics may rhyme; everything you can see, hear or read is new.\n\n**Taste rule:** no jokes about a real person's weight, body shape or eating, and no health claims. Ounce judges wobble, knees, posture and life choices, never bodies. The game asks for no real health data. The About screen says: \"This is not medical advice. Ounce is a scale.\"\n\n---\n\n## 2. World and cast\n\n**Tare Island** (\"Everything here starts at zero.\") is a tiny resort whose landscape is exercise equipment at enormous scale: Kettlebell Volcano (dormant, mostly), Foam Roller Hills, the Dumbbell Bridge, medicine-ball boulders. A white-tiled plaza sits at its heart, ringed by lawn, sand and calm sea.\n\n| Who | What | Voice |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ounce** | The host, a scale. Hops after you on the island. Eyes and a red needle on a round dial: the needle is its eyebrow, and it whips, droops or spins with mood. | High, clipped, polite. \"I'd like to weigh in on that.\" |\n| **Coach Plumb** | 81, silver bun, cyan tracksuit, a plumb bob hanging from one wrist. Demonstrates everything without effort. | Low, slow, deadpan. |\n| **Marguerite** | 74, classmate. Has had everything replaced and tops every leaderboard. Friendly menace. | Warm, unhurried. |\n| **Dennis** | 47, classmate. Here after \"an incident with a deck chair\". Overconfident, always nearly injured. Beatable by Day 3. | Quick, hearty. |\n| **June** | 12, Marguerite's granddaughter. Excellent and bored. | Flat. |\n| **Kevin** | A seagull. Lands on people. Ounce speaks of him as staff. | Squawks. |\n\n**The story in one breath:** you arrive by ferry; Ounce weighs you and gives you an alarming Wobble Age. Through the week the classmates hint that something is off (\"Has anyone ever checked that scale?\"; Coach hangs her plumb line beside Ounce: \"You are leaning.\"; Kevin sits on Ounce and the dial reads 7). On Day 7 you face **The Long Stand**: 90 seconds of standing still while the whole island tries to distract you. Then Ounce confesses: *\"I have been adding seven. To everyone. For years. It felt motivating.\"* The chart of your week visibly slides down seven years. Credits. *\"See you tomorrow. Bring your knees.\"* Play continues as an open-ended Week 2.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Structure of play\n\n### Menu map\n\n```\nTitle (\"Press any button. Gently.\")      idle 10 s on title or any menu -> Attract\n└ Main Menu\n  ├ The Week        pick one of 3 guest slots -> Continue day N / start a week -> Island\n  ├ Drop-In Class   any activity at once, all open, records only, no Drips or story\n  ├ Family Hour     1-4 players on this screen -> Join -> The Medley -> Podium\n  ├ Settings\n  └ About & Credits\n```\n\nIn an AI Hub session none of this appears: the game opens on its own card, then the Medley (section 12).\n\n### Starting a week\n\nName entry, one of 6 preset avatars (the Locker Room changes it later), then the ferry cutscene and Ounce's introduction, which teaches the Dot before the first test: \"This dot is you. Keep it in the circle.\" Then the first Weigh-In. Ounce also asks \"How old do you feel? This is not binding.\" with a number picker; the answer is used once, in the joke when the Wobble Age arrives, and never saved.\n\n### A day in The Week\n\n1. **Morning Weigh-In** (about 45 s, section 6): balance test, one absurd question, today's Wobble Age.\n2. **Free time on the island:** walk between stations, do what you like. Coach's prescription (a daily goal) pays a bonus. Ounce follows and comments.\n3. **Hammock** ends the day once you've done at least one activity. Leaving early earns a guilt-trip, never a block.\n4. **Evening Report** (about 20 s): minutes, Drips, stars, the chart, tomorrow's \"concern\", new unlocks. The light goes to dusk (`applySky(engine, skyAt(19))`), then fade to morning.\n\n### The week\n\n| Day | Test | Coach's prescription (+5 Drips) | Opens today | Story beat |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Stillness | Holding Still and one other | Apologetic Tree, Startled Heron, Squats of Regret, Hoop Economics | Ferry. \"Please step on me.\" \"Ow.\" \"This dot is you. Keep it in the circle. That's it. That's the game. Everything else is decoration.\" |\n| 2 | Lean | 2★ in anything | Coconut Header, Apology Lunges, Reluctant Warrior (adds breathing), Locker Room | Dennis explains the deck chair. Nobody asked. |\n| 3 | Chase | Something you haven't done | Step Class, Slackline, The Chair Nobody Asked For | Marguerite: \"Has anyone ever checked that scale?\" Ounce: \"I'd like to weigh in on that. No.\" |\n| 4 | Stillness | 3 activities | Vitamin Tray, Overhead Mortgage, Tax Season | Coach hangs her plumb line by Ounce. \"You are leaning.\" \"The island is leaning.\" |\n| 5 | Lean | Beat Dennis at anything | Island Jog, Pier Jump, Plank Negotiation | Kevin sits on Ounce. The dial reads 7. Nobody says anything. |\n| 6 | Chase | 3★ anywhere | Hard versions of everything | Ounce rehearses a speech, badly, behind the Sweat Jar. |\n| 7 | Stillness | Stand. | The Long Stand, then the ending | The confession. The chart slides. Credits. |\n\n### Progress\n\n- **Stars:** every activity gives 0-3★ from thresholds in its data.\n- **Drips:** `2 + 3 × stars + floor(playSeconds / 30)`, plus the +5 goal bonus. They fill the **Sweat Jar**.\n- **Jar milestones** at 10, 25, 45, 70, 100, 135 and 175 Drips each unlock a Locker Room item (second headband, towel cape, \"Confidence: no visible effect\", and so on) and a remark.\n- **Records:** per activity, compared with data-table scores for Dennis (low), June (mid-high) and Marguerite (high). A classmate you beat reacts in the Report.\n- **Saves:** `localStorage`, versioned JSON, one key, 3 guest slots, written after every activity and day. Handle missing storage (Node, private mode) with an in-memory fallback.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The core: the Dot\n\nEverything hangs on this feeling good, so it comes first. A stick mapped straight to position would be trivial, because you would just hold still. So the Dot is an unstable weight: you apply force, and it drifts, sways and overshoots.\n\n### The model ✔ (simulated with human-like bots; these are the defaults)\n\n```js\n// src/core/dot.js: pure, no engine imports. Step in fixedUpdate at 60 Hz. p is in -1.2..1.2.\nexport class Dot {\n  x = 0; y = 0; vx = 0; vy = 0; ux = 0; uy = 0; t = 0;\n  constructor(rand) { this.ph = [rand(), rand(), rand(), rand()].map((r) => r * 6.283); }\n  step(dt, inX, inY, T, m = {}) { // T: tunables. m: per-activity modifiers.\n    this.t += dt;\n    const k = Math.min(1, dt / T.dotLag);\n    this.ux += (inX - this.ux) * k; this.uy += (inY - this.uy) * k;\n    const n = T.dotNoise * (m.noise ?? 1), inst = T.dotInstability * (m.inst ?? 1), auth = T.dotAuthority * (m.auth ?? 1);\n    const nx = n * (Math.sin(1.7 * this.t + this.ph[0]) + 0.6 * Math.sin(3.1 * this.t + this.ph[1]));\n    const ny = n * (Math.sin(1.3 * this.t + this.ph[2]) + 0.6 * Math.sin(2.7 * this.t + this.ph[3]));\n    this.vx += (inst * this.x + auth * this.ux - T.dotDamping * this.vx + nx + (m.bx ?? 0)) * dt;\n    this.vy += (inst * this.y + auth * this.uy - T.dotDamping * this.vy + ny + (m.by ?? 0)) * dt;\n    this.x += this.vx * dt; this.y += this.vy * dt;\n    const d = Math.hypot(this.x, this.y);\n    if (d > 1.2) { const s = 1.2 / d; this.x *= s; this.y *= s; this.vx *= 0.3; this.vy *= 0.3; }\n  }\n}\n// Defaults: dotInstability 0.6, dotAuthority 3.0, dotDamping 3.4, dotNoise 0.8, dotLag 0.08\n```\n\nTop speed is about 0.9 units/s, so a lean feels like shifting a heavy marble. Impulses (a sneeze, a gust) add directly to `vx`/`vy`.\n\n### Lean input\n\nAll devices produce one vector `u` in the unit disc, read per lane through a **driver** (section 16).\n\n| Device | `u` |\n|---|---|\n| Stick | `ctx.input.moveAxis()`; set `input.gamepadDeadzone = 0.12` |\n| Keyboard | WASD / arrows, smoothed (attack 0.10 s, release 0.06 s). **Hold Shift for half lean.** |\n| Mouse | Pointer offset from the dial's centre ÷ `mouseLeanRadius` (70 art px), clamped. It is a force, not a target, so you still have to balance. Draw a small crosshair and a line from dial to pointer. |\n| Bot | Delayed PD controller (below) |\n\n### Bots ✔\n\nThe same bots drive classmates, attract mode and the tests:\n\n```\neach step: seen = state tau seconds ago;  d += (rand-0.5)*err*dt*6;  d *= 0.995;\n           u = -kp*(seen.p - target) - kd*seen.v + d;  clamp to the unit disc\nsloppy  tau .42 kp .7  kd .12 err 1.3\naverage tau .30 kp 1.1 kd .35 err .8\ngood    tau .22 kp 1.5 kd .55 err .45\nexpert  tau .17 kp 1.9 kd .7  err .2\n```\n\n**Measured time in ring ✔** (35 s, 40 seeds, first 2 s not counted):\n\n| Ring radius | .40 easy | .30 normal | .22 hard | .16 brutal |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| idle | 11% | 7% | 4% | 2% |\n| sloppy | 21% | 13% | 7% | 4% |\n| average | 71% | 51% | 31% | 18% |\n| good | 100% | 93% | 74% | 49% |\n| expert | 100% | 100% | 98% | 79% |\n| keyboard, average | 88% | 70% | 47% | 28% |\n| mouse, average | 95% | 82% | 59% | 36% |\n\n**Stars for Dot scores: 45 / 70 / 90.** So at ring .30, doing nothing gets no stars, an average player ★, a good one ★★★. Keep this shape if you retune; the test suite guards it (section 18).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Activities\n\n### Shared flow (`src/scenes/activity/base.js`)\n\n`intro` (camera glide, Coach's one line, a card with goal and controls for the current device) → `practice` (first time only: 8 s, unscored, \"Try it.\") → `countdown` → `play` → `outro` → **Results** (stars pop in one by one, score, Drips, a line chosen by activity and stars, classmates' scores) → Retry / Next / Back.\n\n- Each activity is a scene plus a data entry in `src/data/activities.js`: id, name, station, camera, song, tunables, star thresholds, unlock day, `hard` overrides, lines.\n- No fail states. Falls cost points and dignity.\n- **Each activity defines its bot play for a given skill** (circling at a set speed, pressing within a timing error, heading the nearest coconut). The Dot bots in section 4 cover the balance parts. Classmates, attract mode and the tests all use it.\n- Every activity must be readable within 5 seconds with no text.\n- **Hard versions** apply data overrides (smaller rings, faster tempo).\n- **Lanes:** Holding Still, Reps, Hoop, Header and Step run in **lane staging**, up to 4 mats in a row, each with its own driver (local, bot or ghost). Solo play puts you on the centre mat with two classmates as bots.\n\n**Lane camera ✔ (3 mats):** `{ preset: 'fixed', position: [0, 2.3, 8.2], target: [0, 1.15, 0], fov: 30 }`, mats at x = -2.2, 0, 2.2, z = 0.8. **For 4 mats ✔:** position `[0, 3, 9.5]`, target `[0, 1, 0]`, fov 32, mats at x = -3, -1, 1, 3. Multiply the distance by `max(1, (16/9) / aspect)` so narrow windows don't clip the outer mats.\n\n**Staging rules ✔ (found in test renders):**\n- Put Coach to one side of the player, not directly behind; their silhouettes merged.\n- Never draw the dial over an avatar. In 3-mat staging the sky above the player's head is empty: centre the dial at (W/2, 56), radius 44.\n- Make all HUD lines at least 2 art px thick. A 1 px ring vanishes at 10 feet.\n\n**Lean body language:** after `mixer.update(dt)`, add the Dot to the pose: `torso.rotateZ(-dot.x * 0.18); torso.rotateX(-dot.y * 0.12); pelvis.position.x += dot.x * 0.05`. Outside the ring, add a tremble. Far outside, blend to the kit's `Teeter` clip. The player should be able to read the Dot from the body alone.\n\n### 5.1 Holding Still (Pavilion, yoga)\n\nCoach demonstrates on a dais. You hold the pose by keeping the Dot in the ring: settle 4 s (unscored), hold, release 2 s.\n\n| Pose | Clip | Ring | Twist | Hold | Breath |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Apologetic Tree | Tree | .40 | none | 25 s | none |\n| Startled Heron | Heron | .36 | slow bias wander, ±.12 on x | 25 s | none |\n| Reluctant Warrior | Warrior | .32 | y noise ×1.3 | 30 s | 8 s |\n| The Chair Nobody Asked For | Chair | .30 | noise ramps 1→1.5 | 30 s | 8 s |\n| Tax Season | Fold | .28 | bias wander ±.18 | 30 s | 6 s |\n| Plank Negotiation | Plank | .24 | inst ×1.2, tremor after 20 s | 35 s | 6 s |\n\n- **Breath:** a bar rises and falls (\"In…\" / \"Out…\"). Hold R2 / Space / left mouse on In, release on Out. Keep a sync value `s` (0..1, smoothed), and `m.noise *= 1.25 - 0.55 * s`, so breathing well literally calms you.\n- **Score:** `100 × inRing`, or `100 × (0.8 × inRing + 0.2 × breath)` when breath is on. Stars 45/70/90.\n- **Juice:** chime when the Dot enters the ring, ring brightens with time inside, slow camera push-in while steady, classmates wobbling, Kevin on the rail, sunbeams, dust motes.\n\n### 5.2 The Rep Shed (strength)\n\n**You scrub the animation with the stick** ✔ (`action.paused = true; action.time = r * duration; mixer.update(0)`). A marker on a big vertical (or horizontal) track follows the tempo curve; you match it.\n\n| Exercise | Clip | Input | Reps | Tempo |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Squats of Regret | SquatRep | stick down = deeper | 10 | 3.2 s |\n| Apology Lunges | LungeRep, mirrored for left | stick left/right, targets alternate | 10 | 3.2 s |\n| Overhead Mortgage | Press (new) | stick up = press; `repLag` ×1.5 (heavy) | 8 | 3.6 s |\n\n- **Target per rep:** 0→1 in 40% of the tempo, hold 15%, 1→0 in 35%, rest 10%. `r` follows the input with lag `repLag` 0.10 s.\n- **Keyboard uses rate control:** S/W move `r` at the target speed, no key holds it. Otherwise keys could only slam between ends.\n- **Rep quality:** `1 - 1.6 × mean|r - target|`, shown as Perfect ≥ .85, Good ≥ .65, else Wobbly. Score = mean × 100. Stars 55/75/90.\n- **Fatigue:** tremor on `r`, amplitude `repFatigue × (rep/total)²`, default .10.\n- **The gag:** Coach says \"Last one.\" Then \"Last one.\" Then \"I lied. This is the last one.\" Every exercise runs 2 reps past its announced end.\n- **Set dressing:** a gym wall with the engine's `Mirror` showing your reps (attach in `setup`, stop in `dispose`); giant dumbbells.\n\n### 5.3 Hoop Economics (Beat Beach, aerobics)\n\n**Circle the stick** (or the mouse, or roll W-D-S-A) to keep hoops up.\n\n- Angular speed: from unwrapped `atan2(u)` when `|u| > .5`, smoothed .25 s. Band: `hoopLow` 0.9 to `hoopHigh` 2.4 rev/s, in the current direction.\n- Lift `L`: +.35/s in band, -.25/s out of band, -.5/s wrong way. At 0 the hoop drops, and you pick it up for 1.5 s, with comment.\n- 60 s: anticlockwise 25 s; \"Now the other way.\" clockwise 25 s; either way 10 s.\n- **Thrown hoops:** every ~9 s June winds up on one side, with 1.5 s of telegraph and a landing shadow. Flick `|u.x| > .7` toward that side in the last .4 s to catch it. Up to 4 hoops, each adding +0.5 to the rotation multiplier. Flicking interrupts your circling, which is the trade-off.\n- Score = weighted rotations in band. Stars 35/70/110.\n- **Look:** torus meshes orbiting the hips, tilt by `L`, pelvis circling, sparkles in band.\n\n### 5.4 Step Class (Beat Beach, rhythm)\n\nFootprints scroll to a hit line. Left foot: L1 or L2 / F or ← / left mouse button. Right foot: R1 or R2 / J or → / right mouse button.\n\n- 104 BPM, 32 bars (about 74 s): intro 4, verse 8, chorus 8, breakdown 4, final 8. Charts are strings in data: `L.R.L.R.`.\n- Windows: Perfect ±55 ms, Good ±100, OK ±140 (scale `stepWindow`). Accuracy = (P + .7G + .4O) / N. Stars 55/75/90.\n- **Beat clock:** the song's step clock when audio runs (`ctx.audio.song.start` and `ctx.audio.song.parsed.stepTime`, in the audio context's time; undocumented but present in 0.46.0), otherwise game time (headless, replays). A **Step timing offset** setting covers latency.\n- On pause: stop the music; on resume, count in and restart at the current bar.\n- Avatars step on platforms using the kit's `StepUp` / `StepDown` or your own. Classmates dance with occasional mistakes. Confetti at combo milestones.\n\n### 5.5 Coconut Header (Lagoon Games, balance)\n\nLean left and right along the sand. Dennis runs a catapult.\n\n- Lane ±2 m solo (±0.8 m in lanes). Speed 4.5 m/s × `u.x`.\n- Objects land at head height after 1.4 s with a shadow telegraph, interval 1.3 s → 0.7 s. 70% coconut (head it: +10 × combo tier), 25% flip-flop (dodge it, or -10, combo lost, .6 s daze with circling stars), 5% golden pineapple (+30).\n- Combo tiers ×2/×3/×4 at 5/10/20. 60 s. Stars 150/400/800.\n- Hitstop .05 s on each header. Squash the head. Coconuts fly back at Dennis, who stops being fine.\n\n### 5.6 Slackline (Lagoon Games, balance)\n\nA sagging line across the lagoon between giant dumbbells. Camera low behind you.\n\n- **X is balance, forward is progress.** Push forward (`u.y > .3`) to walk at 1.1 m/s. Walking is less stable, so stopping to recover is a real choice.\n- Defaults ✔ (simulated): effective instability 1.2 standing and 2.6 walking, so `m.inst = 1.2 / T.dotInstability` and `T.slackWalkInst / T.dotInstability`; `m.noise` 1.25; gusts every 6 m, `m.bx` ±1.6 for 1 s, telegraphed 1.5 s by flags and wind lines; fall at `|x| > 1`. Lock y (`dot.y = dot.vy = 0` after each step). Bots walk only while `|x| < .45`.\n- Fall: 1.2 s of slow motion, splash, back to the checkpoint (0/10/20 m). Kevin lands on your head at 18 m and adds a bias until 24 m. 30 m, 75 s limit.\n- Measured ✔: idle never finishes; sloppy rarely; average 38 s with 0.2 falls; good 28 s with 0 falls.\n- **Stars:** ★ finish; ★★ ≤ 1 fall and under 45 s; ★★★ 0 falls and under 32 s. Score `1000 - 60 × falls - 8 × (time - 27)`.\n- Sag the line under your feet (segments updated each frame). Arms out: the kit's `Tiptoe` plus T-pose arms set after the mixer, or a tuned `gaitClip`.\n\n### 5.7 Vitamin Tray (Lagoon Games, balance)\n\nTilt a lacquered tray to roll coloured vitamins into matching cups. Top-down-ish fixed camera.\n\n- **Tilt gravity, not the tray ✔:** the colliders stay flat and static; set `ctx.physics.world.gravity`; a visual `Group` tilts, with the vitamins as its children at `body.translation()`.\n- **The engine's gravity is -24**, not -9.8. Use `trayGravity` 9.8 here and restore it on leaving. Max tilt 14°, driven by `u` with .12 s lag. Enable CCD on the balls.\n- Cups are untagged cylinder triggers ✔ (they see dynamic bodies). 8 vitamins, 2-3 colours, 60 s. +10 correct, -3 wrong; rail gaps in later boards drop a vitamin (-1, respawns after 3 s).\n- Three boards as ASCII in data (`#` wall, `.` floor, `o` start, `R`/`B`/`Y` cups). Stars at 40/60/80% of the board's maximum.\n\n### 5.8 Island Jog (Jogging Path, aerobics)\n\nA rail run around the whole island, the showpiece for the world. `iso` camera following, zoom about 1.4, looking slightly ahead.\n\n- **Alternate left and right** (same inputs as Step) to build cadence (smoothed steps/s). Band 2.4-3.6 steps/s. Same side twice stumbles (-.5 s). Over 3.6 for 2 s gives a Stitch (speed ×.5 for 3 s, remark).\n- Coach paces you on a white mobility scooter at exactly target speed, 3 m/s. Path about 220 m on a Catmull-Rom curve (about 75 s). More than 12 m behind, she waits, unimpressed.\n- Stick X moves you ±1.5 m across the path: collect 18 water cups; dodge sprinklers, picnics, Dennis stretching, and Kevin.\n- Score `70 × pace% + 30 × cups%`. Stars 55/75/90.\n- Run clip: `gaitClip(HERO_RIG, { name: 'Jog', frames: 16, speed: 3, stance: .42, hip: -.04, bob: .03, lift: .1, lean: 6, sway: 2, arms: { swing: 30, elbow: 75 } })` ✔, with `action.timeScale = speed / 3`.\n\n### 5.9 Pier Jump (Lagoon Games, balance)\n\nSide-on (`side` preset), three attempts, three judges with paddles.\n\n1. **Run-up (5 s):** hold the Dot in a tuck zone (`y` -.8 to -.4) to build speed 6→14 m/s.\n2. **Lip:** press ✕ / Space / click as you reach it. Power `p = 1 - |Δt| / .25`.\n3. **Flight (3-4 s):** `m.inst` 3, ring .45; staying in the ring carries you farther.\n4. **Cannonball:** press in the last .5 s for style points. Splash: hitstop .1 s, `timeScale` .25 for .8 s, huge particles, ripples, shake.\n\nDistance = `speed × (.8 + .6p) × (.6 + .4 × ringFrac) × 2.14` m. Stars at 18/28/38 m on the best attempt. Judges react.\n\n### 5.10 The Long Stand (finale, Day 7)\n\nThe plaza at golden hour, everyone watching. Stillness scoring with this data timeline; no way to fail:\n\n| t (s) | Event | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | Coach: \"Stand.\" | ring .40 |\n| 10 | Kevin lands on your head | bias x +.10 |\n| 20 | Dennis sneezes | impulse y -.6 |\n| 30 | | ring .34 |\n| 35 | Light rain (`Precipitation`) | noise ×1.2 |\n| 45 | Marguerite applauds, slowly | Ounce: \"Ignore her.\" |\n| 50 | | ring .28 |\n| 55 | June: \"Are you nearly done?\" | text drifts across the dial |\n| 65 | Kettlebell Volcano rumbles | shake, random impulse |\n| 75 | Rain stops, sun out, music swells | ring .24 |\n| 85 | Kevin leaves | bias off, impulse |\n| 90 | Coach: \"You may blink.\" | end |\n\nThen the confession and ending, played with a `Timeline` (section 13). At 0★: \"You stood. Sort of.\"\n\n---\n\n## 6. Weigh-In and Wobble Age\n\nYour avatar steps onto Ounce (\"Ow.\" every time; the delivery varies). Close-up, huge dial, tiny animated needle.\n\n| Test | Rules (12-15 s) | Result T (0..1) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stillness | Ring .30. Dot and ring fade after 3 s: \"Eyes closed.\" | in-ring fraction |\n| Lean | Three targets (\"70% left\"). Reach and hold ±.1 for 1.5 s each. | based on time |\n| Chase | Follow a slow target on a Lissajous path | 1 - mean distance × 2 |\n\nThen one question (section 8), then the reveal: the needle spins, the number rolls, Ounce remarks.\n\n```\ntrue  = round(20 + 60 × (1 - mean(last 3 T))) - min(20, totalStars × 0.5), clamped to 18..95\nshown = true + 7 until the confession, then true\n```\n\nThe chart draws 7 points. At the confession every point animates down by 7. Test it: an expert bot ends lower than an average one, an idle bot stays high, and the confession subtracts exactly 7.\n\n---\n\n## 7. The island hub\n\n`iso` camera ✔ (`{ preset: 'iso', zoom: 1.15 }`) with `PlatformerCharacter` and the kit's full moveset. Jumping and waving have no purpose and should still be there. Island about 44 × 34 m. The top of the screen is -x, -z: **put tall things in that quadrant** so they never hide the player.\n\n| Place | Near (x, z) | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Weigh-In Terrace | (0, 3) | Day start, Ounce's dais |\n| Pavilion of Holding Still | (-11, 3), sky-blue roof | Poses |\n| The Rep Shed | (11, 3), orange roof | Reps |\n| Beat Beach | (12, -10), on sand | Hoop, Step |\n| Lagoon Games | (-12, -10), pier | Header, Slackline, Tray, Pier Jump |\n| Jogging Path gate | (0, 11) | Island Jog |\n| Sweat Jar | (4, -4) | Drips, milestones |\n| Locker Room | (-4, -4) | Avatar |\n| Hall of Numbers | (0, -8) | Records, chart, calendar |\n| Hammock between palms | (14, 10) | End the day |\n| Scenery | | Kettlebell Volcano (-15, -14), Foam Roller Hills (15, -14), Dumbbell Bridge over an inlet, medicine balls, gulls (`Boids`) overhead |\n\n- **Interaction:** a trigger shows `[✕] Enter: Pavilion of Holding Still`. Confirm opens a station menu: activities with stars and best score, locked rows greyed with the unlock day, one remark.\n- **Mouse:** hovering a station highlights it and names it; clicking walks there and enters; clicking ground walks there (pointer ray via `camera.rayAt`, then `castRay`). Steer the hero by writing its `MoveInput.move`.\n- **HUD:** day top-left, Drips top-right, today's prescription under the day, prompts bottom. Nothing else.\n- **Ounce** hops after you (`Squash`, tween) and barks at most every 6 s.\n- **Life:** classmates around the island doing small loops (stretching, sitting, failing).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Writing\n\n**Voice:** short declarative sentences. Polite, clinical, caring, faintly wounding. A remark should sound like it came from a nurse who has read your file. Understatement over exclamation. Numbers delivered gently. Humour comes from what is implied. No sarcasm tags, no memes, no pop-culture references, no swearing. Respect the taste rule in section 1.\n\n**Format:** at most 3 lines of 52 characters. Write at least 120 lines beyond the samples: every activity needs lines for 0/1/2/3★, first time and repeat; plus barks for ring entry and exit, falls and streaks.\n\n**Samples, in voice. Use them, then write more like them.**\n\n*Ounce:*\n- \"Hello! I'm Ounce. Please step on me.\" / \"Ow.\" / \"That was a joke. I can't feel pain. I can only keep records.\"\n- \"How old do you feel? This is not binding.\"\n- \"Your Wobble Age is 68. Is that close? No? I'm so sorry.\"\n- \"Thank you. I have written that down.\"\n- \"You wobbled 14 times. I only counted the ones you noticed.\"\n- \"Your left side is doing very well. I'll let your right side know.\"\n- \"No change since yesterday. Which is, in a way, consistent.\"\n- \"Excellent! ...Compared to Dennis.\"\n- \"I'm not angry. Scales don't get angry. We get accurate.\"\n- \"Have you been stretching? Please don't lie. I'm very sensitive.\"\n- \"Today's concern: your ankles. I've been concerned all night.\"\n- \"Good morning. You look rested. I can't see. But you felt rested.\"\n- \"Kevin has been with us eleven years. He has never once held a pose.\"\n- \"This is a recording. The person in it is doing better than you.\" *(attract mode)*\n- \"I have been adding seven. To everyone. For years. It felt motivating.\"\n- \"See you tomorrow. Bring your knees.\"\n\n*Coach Plumb:*\n- \"You are a tree. A tree does not check its phone.\"\n- \"Your left knee has wandered off. Please collect it.\"\n- \"Breathe in. Not like that.\"\n- \"Good. Again. That was also good. Again.\"\n- \"Hold. You may blink on Thursday.\"\n- \"I am eighty-one. I say this so that you feel something.\"\n- \"Stillness is a muscle. So is regret. We train both here.\"\n- \"The floor is your friend. It has never once let go.\"\n\n*Classmates:*\n- Marguerite: \"Both hips, new. Knee, new. Opinions, original.\"\n- Dennis: \"I used to do this professionally. Not this. Something near it.\"\n- Dennis: \"It's fine. It's just the one leg.\"\n- June: \"I'm pacing myself.\"\n- June: \"My gran says you're doing your best. She said it very slowly.\"\n\n**Morning questions** (three answers; they change only the remark, and Ounce files them):\n- When you stand up, what sound do you make? *Oof. / Hnng. / I have become the sound.*\n- Have you ever apologised to a chair? *Yes. / Not yet. / It started it.*\n- How many stairs is too many? *One. / All of them. / Depends who's watching.*\n- How do you feel about your ankles? *Fine. / We don't speak. / Which ones?*\n- What is your relationship with the floor? *Distant. / Improving. / It's complicated.*\n- If your knees could talk, what would they say? *Please. / Why. / Nothing. They've given up.*\n- Day 7: Are you happy? *Yes. / No. / Is this graded?","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-build-v2--20261105-012138--x6981e3-work/d022da92-c114-4f47-a8f5-ca68c59e3f52/tool-results/b7q8ktfej.txt","persistedOutputSize":87980},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Edqd4LLw6tsb7q8JKTPbUy","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjTTKsKGpDRsMBBBbdcc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012p2yzhLSw4QccVk7mYNxmn","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-012138__x6981e3/work/TASK.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1061,"cache_read_input_tokens":19165,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1061},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d022da92-c114-4f47-a8f5-ca68c59e3f52","uuid":"210a0e2c-eed1-4fa2-875a-350b3f13e4a1","timestamp":"2026-11-05T08:21:47.298Z","request_id":"req_011CgjTTKeS1iWhaJixMjCjY","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_012p2yzhLSw4QccVk7mYNxmn":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-012138__x6981e3/work/TASK.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012p2yzhLSw4QccVk7mYNxmn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tSOURCE: Wii Fit\n2\t\n3\tGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n4\t\n5\tPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n6\t\n7\tGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n8\t\n9\tResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n10\t\n11\tPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-012138].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n12\t\n13\tTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, the riftlight engine kit. Keep the engine (pixel-engine.js) as it ships, as its guide says, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n14\t\n15\tArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n16\t\n17\tAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n18\t\n19\tAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n20\t\n21\tOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n22\t\n23\tWorking Environment and deliverable:\n24\tCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-012138] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-012138].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n25\t\n26\tPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n27\t\n28\tTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n29\t\n30\tEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n31\t\n32\t## This computer\n33\tChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n34\t\n35\t- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n36\t- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n37\t- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n38\t- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n39\t  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n40\t  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n41\t  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n42\t- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n43\t- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n44\t- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n45\t- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n46\t- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n47\t\n48\t### What this computer doesn't have\n49\t\n50\tNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n51\t\n52\t- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n53\t- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n54\t- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n55\t- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n56\t- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n57\t- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n58\t\n59\t### Sharing this computer\n60\t\n61\t- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n62\t- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n63\t- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n64\t- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n65\t\n66\t## Goal\n67\t\n68\tBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n69\t\n70\tScope\n71\t\n72\tCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n73\t\n74\tInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n75\t\n76\tAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n77\t\n78\tMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n79\t\n80\tNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n81\t\n82\t## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\n83\tDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n84\t\n85\tDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n86\t\n87\tTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n88\t\n89\t## Done\n90\tNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n91\t\n92\t## Source Information\n93\tCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n94\t- Date: 2026-11-05 01:21:38\n95\t- Source: the work it's based on\n96\t- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n97\t- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n98\t- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n99\t- Art Direction: style used\n100\t- Animation: system used\n101\t- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n102\t- Online: network features used, or none\n103\t- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n104\t- Research: research done, or none\n105\t- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n106\t- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n107\t- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n108\t\n109\tExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n110\t{\n111\t  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n112\t  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n113\t  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n114\t  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n115\t  \"aiHub\": {\n116\t    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n117\t    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n118\t    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n119\t    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n120\t    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n121\t    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n122\t    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n123\t    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n124\t    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n125\t    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n126\t  }\n127\t}\n128\tThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n129\t\n130\tTesting Feedback\n131\t\n132\t- How many issues did testing catch?\n133\t- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n134\t- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n135\t- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n136\t\n137\tEngine Feedback\n138\t\n139\t- What did you like most about the engine?\n140\t- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n141\t- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n142\t- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n143\t- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n144\t- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n145\t\n146\tPrompt Feedback\n147\t\n148\t- What did you like most about this prompt?\n149\t- What was confusing?\n150\t- What was the hardest thing to do?\n151\t- What would you change in this prompt?\n152\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d022da92-c114-4f47-a8f5-ca68c59e3f52","uuid":"7eb5d91c-a8b3-4c5a-83e2-e7b85e81335f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T08:21:47.313Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-012138__x6981e3/work/TASK.md","content":"SOURCE: Wii Fit\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-012138].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, the riftlight engine kit. Keep the engine (pixel-engine.js) as it ships, as its guide says, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-012138] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-012138].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## This computer\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n\n### What this computer doesn't have\n\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n### Sharing this computer\n\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-05 01:21:38\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n","numLines":152,"startLine":1,"totalLines":152}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012p2yzhLSw4QccVk7mYNxmn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjTTKsKGpDRsMBBBbdcc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FRvGYj4iK4UAvttBpud8Sx","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-012138__x6981e3/work/PLAN.md","limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1061,"cache_read_input_tokens":19165,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1061},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d022da92-c114-4f47-a8f5-ca68c59e3f52","uuid":"24de3638-dc4a-4716-a760-2fb25bde7f96","timestamp":"2026-11-05T08:21:47.483Z","request_id":"req_011CgjTTKeS1iWhaJixMjCjY","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01FRvGYj4iK4UAvttBpud8Sx":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-012138__x6981e3/work/PLAN.md","limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FRvGYj4iK4UAvttBpud8Sx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Game plan: an original fitness comedy inspired by Wii Fit\n2\t\n3\tYou are the builder. Build this game from this plan and your brief. Nobody will answer questions, so where the plan leaves something open, decide and move on.\n4\t\n5\t- **The title is yours.** Pick it the way your brief says. It lives in one constant (`src/data/title.js`) and nothing else hard-codes it. In this plan `{TITLE}` stands for it.\n6\t- **Paths are relative to your dev folder.** \"Root\" means its parent, where your brief puts the deliverable html and the exit-interview file. Use the names your brief gives.\n7\t- **✔ means the director ran it** on this machine against engine kit 0.46.0 and hub client 1.4.0, in the form written here. Don't re-derive it. If your download is newer, read its changelog from 0.46.0 on.\n8\t- **Read in this order:** this plan; the kit's `GUIDE.md` and `ASSETS.md`, whole; the hub's `README.md`, whole. Search `API.md` and `LIBRARY.md`; don't read them whole.\n9\t- Your brief's rules on folders, ports, processes, browsers and test reporting still apply. They are repeated here only where they shape the work.\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t## 1. The game\n14\t\n15\t**A week at a spotless wellness island run by a small, polite, deeply judgmental bathroom scale. Your whole body is one dot. Keep it in the circle.**\n16\t\n17\t- **Genre:** fitness and balance minigame collection, inside a light life-sim week. (Catalogue genre: Sports.)\n18\t- **Perspective:** isometric on the island; fixed front-on for class activities; other fixed views per activity (section 5).\n19\t- **Length:** a story week of 7 short days, 16 minutes at the quickest and about an hour for a player who tries everything. Everything is also playable at once from Drop-In Class.\n20\t\n21\t### Pillars (use these to settle any open question)\n22\t\n23\t1. **You are a dot.** Almost everything is one verb: lean. The left stick, WASD or the mouse moves your centre of balance, and it has weight and sway. Poses, reps, hoops, slacklines and the finale are all different ways of moving or holding the Dot.\n24\t2. **Politely judged.** Everything you do gets a calm, clinical, faintly wounding remark. You cannot lose. You can only be noted.\n25\t3. **One small day at a time.** Short sessions, a morning number, a chart, a jar slowly filling.\n26\t4. **Always in class.** Other people are always beside you: classmates alone, family on the couch, friends as ghosts online. Everyone wobbles.\n27\t\n28\t### What it takes from the source, and how it stays original\n29\t\n30\t| Distinctive in the source | Here |\n31\t|---|---|\n32\t| Your body is the controller; a centre-of-balance dot | The Dot: real sway physics on a stick, keys or mouse |\n33\t| A talking device that politely judges you | Ounce, a mint retro dial scale whose dial is its face |\n34\t| A calm instructor demonstrating | Coach Plumb, 81, unblinking, never off vertical |\n35\t| Daily check-in, one number that sums you up, a graph | Weigh-In, Wobble Age, a line chart with a twist |\n36\t| Yoga, strength, aerobics, balance games | Four stations on the island |\n37\t| Minutes banked toward unlocks | Drips in the Sweat Jar |\n38\t| A bright, clean, soft-spoken world | White tile plaza on a pastel island made of giant gym equipment |\n39\t| A household sharing one device | Family Hour on one TV, AI Hub sessions online |\n40\t\n41\t**Do not use** any Nintendo name, character, look, music, level or minigame name: no Wii, Mii, Balance Board, Wii Fit Trainer, Wuhu Island or Fit Age. No white-board host, no sound-alike tunes, no channel-style menus. Mechanics may rhyme; everything you can see, hear or read is new.\n42\t\n43\t**Taste rule:** no jokes about a real person's weight, body shape or eating, and no health claims. Ounce judges wobble, knees, posture and life choices, never bodies. The game asks for no real health data. The About screen says: \"This is not medical advice. Ounce is a scale.\"\n44\t\n45\t---\n46\t\n47\t## 2. World and cast\n48\t\n49\t**Tare Island** (\"Everything here starts at zero.\") is a tiny resort whose landscape is exercise equipment at enormous scale: Kettlebell Volcano (dormant, mostly), Foam Roller Hills, the Dumbbell Bridge, medicine-ball boulders. A white-tiled plaza sits at its heart, ringed by lawn, sand and calm sea.\n50\t\n51\t| Who | What | Voice |\n52\t|---|---|---|\n53\t| **Ounce** | The host, a scale. Hops after you on the island. Eyes and a red needle on a round dial: the needle is its eyebrow, and it whips, droops or spins with mood. | High, clipped, polite. \"I'd like to weigh in on that.\" |\n54\t| **Coach Plumb** | 81, silver bun, cyan tracksuit, a plumb bob hanging from one wrist. Demonstrates everything without effort. | Low, slow, deadpan. |\n55\t| **Marguerite** | 74, classmate. Has had everything replaced and tops every leaderboard. Friendly menace. | Warm, unhurried. |\n56\t| **Dennis** | 47, classmate. Here after \"an incident with a deck chair\". Overconfident, always nearly injured. Beatable by Day 3. | Quick, hearty. |\n57\t| **June** | 12, Marguerite's granddaughter. Excellent and bored. | Flat. |\n58\t| **Kevin** | A seagull. Lands on people. Ounce speaks of him as staff. | Squawks. |\n59\t\n60\t**The story in one breath:** you arrive by ferry; Ounce weighs you and gives you an alarming Wobble Age. Through the week the classmates hint that something is off (\"Has anyone ever checked that scale?\"; Coach hangs her plumb line beside Ounce: \"You are leaning.\"; Kevin sits on Ounce and the dial reads 7). On Day 7 you face **The Long Stand**: 90 seconds of standing still while the whole island tries to distract you. Then Ounce confesses: *\"I have been adding seven. To everyone. For years. It felt motivating.\"* The chart of your week visibly slides down seven years. Credits. *\"See you tomorrow. Bring your knees.\"* Play continues as an open-ended Week 2.\n61\t\n62\t---\n63\t\n64\t## 3. Structure of play\n65\t\n66\t### Menu map\n67\t\n68\t```\n69\tTitle (\"Press any button. Gently.\")      idle 10 s on title or any menu -> Attract\n70\t└ Main Menu\n71\t  ├ The Week        pick one of 3 guest slots -> Continue day N / start a week -> Island\n72\t  ├ Drop-In Class   any activity at once, all open, records only, no Drips or story\n73\t  ├ Family Hour     1-4 players on this screen -> Join -> The Medley -> Podium\n74\t  ├ Settings\n75\t  └ About & Credits\n76\t```\n77\t\n78\tIn an AI Hub session none of this appears: the game opens on its own card, then the Medley (section 12).\n79\t\n80\t### Starting a week\n81\t\n82\tName entry, one of 6 preset avatars (the Locker Room changes it later), then the ferry cutscene and Ounce's introduction, which teaches the Dot before the first test: \"This dot is you. Keep it in the circle.\" Then the first Weigh-In. Ounce also asks \"How old do you feel? This is not binding.\" with a number picker; the answer is used once, in the joke when the Wobble Age arrives, and never saved.\n83\t\n84\t### A day in The Week\n85\t\n86\t1. **Morning Weigh-In** (about 45 s, section 6): balance test, one absurd question, today's Wobble Age.\n87\t2. **Free time on the island:** walk between stations, do what you like. Coach's prescription (a daily goal) pays a bonus. Ounce follows and comments.\n88\t3. **Hammock** ends the day once you've done at least one activity. Leaving early earns a guilt-trip, never a block.\n89\t4. **Evening Report** (about 20 s): minutes, Drips, stars, the chart, tomorrow's \"concern\", new unlocks. The light goes to dusk (`applySky(engine, skyAt(19))`), then fade to morning.\n90\t\n91\t### The week\n92\t\n93\t| Day | Test | Coach's prescription (+5 Drips) | Opens today | Story beat |\n94\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n95\t| 1 | Stillness | Holding Still and one other | Apologetic Tree, Startled Heron, Squats of Regret, Hoop Economics | Ferry. \"Please step on me.\" \"Ow.\" \"This dot is you. Keep it in the circle. That's it. That's the game. Everything else is decoration.\" |\n96\t| 2 | Lean | 2★ in anything | Coconut Header, Apology Lunges, Reluctant Warrior (adds breathing), Locker Room | Dennis explains the deck chair. Nobody asked. |\n97\t| 3 | Chase | Something you haven't done | Step Class, Slackline, The Chair Nobody Asked For | Marguerite: \"Has anyone ever checked that scale?\" Ounce: \"I'd like to weigh in on that. No.\" |\n98\t| 4 | Stillness | 3 activities | Vitamin Tray, Overhead Mortgage, Tax Season | Coach hangs her plumb line by Ounce. \"You are leaning.\" \"The island is leaning.\" |\n99\t| 5 | Lean | Beat Dennis at anything | Island Jog, Pier Jump, Plank Negotiation | Kevin sits on Ounce. The dial reads 7. Nobody says anything. |\n100\t| 6 | Chase | 3★ anywhere | Hard versions of everything | Ounce rehearses a speech, badly, behind the Sweat Jar. |\n101\t| 7 | Stillness | Stand. | The Long Stand, then the ending | The confession. The chart slides. Credits. |\n102\t\n103\t### Progress\n104\t\n105\t- **Stars:** every activity gives 0-3★ from thresholds in its data.\n106\t- **Drips:** `2 + 3 × stars + floor(playSeconds / 30)`, plus the +5 goal bonus. They fill the **Sweat Jar**.\n107\t- **Jar milestones** at 10, 25, 45, 70, 100, 135 and 175 Drips each unlock a Locker Room item (second headband, towel cape, \"Confidence: no visible effect\", and so on) and a remark.\n108\t- **Records:** per activity, compared with data-table scores for Dennis (low), June (mid-high) and Marguerite (high). A classmate you beat reacts in the Report.\n109\t- **Saves:** `localStorage`, versioned JSON, one key, 3 guest slots, written after every activity and day. Handle missing storage (Node, private mode) with an in-memory fallback.\n110\t\n111\t---\n112\t\n113\t## 4. The core: the Dot\n114\t\n115\tEverything hangs on this feeling good, so it comes first. A stick mapped straight to position would be trivial, because you would just hold still. So the Dot is an unstable weight: you apply force, and it drifts, sways and overshoots.\n116\t\n117\t### The model ✔ (simulated with human-like bots; these are the defaults)\n118\t\n119\t```js\n120\t// src/core/dot.js: pure, no engine imports. Step in fixedUpdate at 60 Hz. p is in -1.2..1.2.\n121\texport class Dot {\n122\t  x = 0; y = 0; vx = 0; vy = 0; ux = 0; uy = 0; t = 0;\n123\t  constructor(rand) { this.ph = [rand(), rand(), rand(), rand()].map((r) => r * 6.283); }\n124\t  step(dt, inX, inY, T, m = {}) { // T: tunables. m: per-activity modifiers.\n125\t    this.t += dt;\n126\t    const k = Math.min(1, dt / T.dotLag);\n127\t    this.ux += (inX - this.ux) * k; this.uy += (inY - this.uy) * k;\n128\t    const n = T.dotNoise * (m.noise ?? 1), inst = T.dotInstability * (m.inst ?? 1), auth = T.dotAuthority * (m.auth ?? 1);\n129\t    const nx = n * (Math.sin(1.7 * this.t + this.ph[0]) + 0.6 * Math.sin(3.1 * this.t + this.ph[1]));\n130\t    const ny = n * (Math.sin(1.3 * this.t + this.ph[2]) + 0.6 * Math.sin(2.7 * this.t + this.ph[3]));\n131\t    this.vx += (inst * this.x + auth * this.ux - T.dotDamping * this.vx + nx + (m.bx ?? 0)) * dt;\n132\t    this.vy += (inst * this.y + auth * this.uy - T.dotDamping * this.vy + ny + (m.by ?? 0)) * dt;\n133\t    this.x += this.vx * dt; this.y += this.vy * dt;\n134\t    const d = Math.hypot(this.x, this.y);\n135\t    if (d > 1.2) { const s = 1.2 / d; this.x *= s; this.y *= s; this.vx *= 0.3; this.vy *= 0.3; }\n136\t  }\n137\t}\n138\t// Defaults: dotInstability 0.6, dotAuthority 3.0, dotDamping 3.4, dotNoise 0.8, dotLag 0.08\n139\t```\n140\t\n141\tTop speed is about 0.9 units/s, so a lean feels like shifting a heavy marble. Impulses (a sneeze, a gust) add directly to `vx`/`vy`.\n142\t\n143\t### Lean input\n144\t\n145\tAll devices produce one vector `u` in the unit disc, read per lane through a **driver** (section 16).\n146\t\n147\t| Device | `u` |\n148\t|---|---|\n149\t| Stick | `ctx.input.moveAxis()`; set `input.gamepadDeadzone = 0.12` |\n150\t| Keyboard | WASD / arrows, smoothed (attack 0.10 s, release 0.06 s). **Hold Shift for half lean.** |\n151\t| Mouse | Pointer offset from the dial's centre ÷ `mouseLeanRadius` (70 art px), clamped. It is a force, not a target, so you still have to balance. Draw a small crosshair and a line from dial to pointer. |\n152\t| Bot | Delayed PD controller (below) |\n153\t\n154\t### Bots ✔\n155\t\n156\tThe same bots drive classmates, attract mode and the tests:\n157\t\n158\t```\n159\teach step: seen = state tau seconds ago;  d += (rand-0.5)*err*dt*6;  d *= 0.995;\n160\t           u = -kp*(seen.p - target) - kd*seen.v + d;  clamp to the unit disc\n161\tsloppy  tau .42 kp .7  kd .12 err 1.3\n162\taverage tau .30 kp 1.1 kd .35 err .8\n163\tgood    tau .22 kp 1.5 kd .55 err .45\n164\texpert  tau .17 kp 1.9 kd .7  err .2\n165\t```\n166\t\n167\t**Measured time in ring ✔** (35 s, 40 seeds, first 2 s not counted):\n168\t\n169\t| Ring radius | .40 easy | .30 normal | .22 hard | .16 brutal |\n170\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n171\t| idle | 11% | 7% | 4% | 2% |\n172\t| sloppy | 21% | 13% | 7% | 4% |\n173\t| average | 71% | 51% | 31% | 18% |\n174\t| good | 100% | 93% | 74% | 49% |\n175\t| expert | 100% | 100% | 98% | 79% |\n176\t| keyboard, average | 88% | 70% | 47% | 28% |\n177\t| mouse, average | 95% | 82% | 59% | 36% |\n178\t\n179\t**Stars for Dot scores: 45 / 70 / 90.** So at ring .30, doing nothing gets no stars, an average player ★, a good one ★★★. Keep this shape if you retune; the test suite guards it (section 18).\n180\t\n181\t---\n182\t\n183\t## 5. Activities\n184\t\n185\t### Shared flow (`src/scenes/activity/base.js`)\n186\t\n187\t`intro` (camera glide, Coach's one line, a card with goal and controls for the current device) → `practice` (first time only: 8 s, unscored, \"Try it.\") → `countdown` → `play` → `outro` → **Results** (stars pop in one by one, score, Drips, a line chosen by activity and stars, classmates' scores) → Retry / Next / Back.\n188\t\n189\t- Each activity is a scene plus a data entry in `src/data/activities.js`: id, name, station, camera, song, tunables, star thresholds, unlock day, `hard` overrides, lines.\n190\t- No fail states. Falls cost points and dignity.\n191\t- **Each activity defines its bot play for a given skill** (circling at a set speed, pressing within a timing error, heading the nearest coconut). The Dot bots in section 4 cover the balance parts. Classmates, attract mode and the tests all use it.\n192\t- Every activity must be readable within 5 seconds with no text.\n193\t- **Hard versions** apply data overrides (smaller rings, faster tempo).\n194\t- **Lanes:** Holding Still, Reps, Hoop, Header and Step run in **lane staging**, up to 4 mats in a row, each with its own driver (local, bot or ghost). Solo play puts you on the centre mat with two classmates as bots.\n195\t\n196\t**Lane camera ✔ (3 mats):** `{ preset: 'fixed', position: [0, 2.3, 8.2], target: [0, 1.15, 0], fov: 30 }`, mats at x = -2.2, 0, 2.2, z = 0.8. **For 4 mats ✔:** position `[0, 3, 9.5]`, target `[0, 1, 0]`, fov 32, mats at x = -3, -1, 1, 3. Multiply the distance by `max(1, (16/9) / aspect)` so narrow windows don't clip the outer mats.\n197\t\n198\t**Staging rules ✔ (found in test renders):**\n199\t- Put Coach to one side of the player, not directly behind; their silhouettes merged.\n200\t- Never draw the dial over an avatar. In 3-mat staging the sky above the player's head is empty: centre the dial at (W/2, 56), radius 44.\n201\t- Make all HUD lines at least 2 art px thick. A 1 px ring vanishes at 10 feet.\n202\t\n203\t**Lean body language:** after `mixer.update(dt)`, add the Dot to the pose: `torso.rotateZ(-dot.x * 0.18); torso.rotateX(-dot.y * 0.12); pelvis.position.x += dot.x * 0.05`. Outside the ring, add a tremble. Far outside, blend to the kit's `Teeter` clip. The player should be able to read the Dot from the body alone.\n204\t\n205\t### 5.1 Holding Still (Pavilion, yoga)\n206\t\n207\tCoach demonstrates on a dais. You hold the pose by keeping the Dot in the ring: settle 4 s (unscored), hold, release 2 s.\n208\t\n209\t| Pose | Clip | Ring | Twist | Hold | Breath |\n210\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n211\t| Apologetic Tree | Tree | .40 | none | 25 s | none |\n212\t| Startled Heron | Heron | .36 | slow bias wander, ±.12 on x | 25 s | none |\n213\t| Reluctant Warrior | Warrior | .32 | y noise ×1.3 | 30 s | 8 s |\n214\t| The Chair Nobody Asked For | Chair | .30 | noise ramps 1→1.5 | 30 s | 8 s |\n215\t| Tax Season | Fold | .28 | bias wander ±.18 | 30 s | 6 s |\n216\t| Plank Negotiation | Plank | .24 | inst ×1.2, tremor after 20 s | 35 s | 6 s |\n217\t\n218\t- **Breath:** a bar rises and falls (\"In…\" / \"Out…\"). Hold R2 / Space / left mouse on In, release on Out. Keep a sync value `s` (0..1, smoothed), and `m.noise *= 1.25 - 0.55 * s`, so breathing well literally calms you.\n219\t- **Score:** `100 × inRing`, or `100 × (0.8 × inRing + 0.2 × breath)` when breath is on. Stars 45/70/90.\n220\t- **Juice:** chime when the Dot enters the ring, ring brightens with time inside, slow camera push-in while steady, classmates wobbling, Kevin on the rail, sunbeams, dust motes.\n221\t\n222\t### 5.2 The Rep Shed (strength)\n223\t\n224\t**You scrub the animation with the stick** ✔ (`action.paused = true; action.time = r * duration; mixer.update(0)`). A marker on a big vertical (or horizontal) track follows the tempo curve; you match it.\n225\t\n226\t| Exercise | Clip | Input | Reps | Tempo |\n227\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n228\t| Squats of Regret | SquatRep | stick down = deeper | 10 | 3.2 s |\n229\t| Apology Lunges | LungeRep, mirrored for left | stick left/right, targets alternate | 10 | 3.2 s |\n230\t| Overhead Mortgage | Press (new) | stick up = press; `repLag` ×1.5 (heavy) | 8 | 3.6 s |\n231\t\n232\t- **Target per rep:** 0→1 in 40% of the tempo, hold 15%, 1→0 in 35%, rest 10%. `r` follows the input with lag `repLag` 0.10 s.\n233\t- **Keyboard uses rate control:** S/W move `r` at the target speed, no key holds it. Otherwise keys could only slam between ends.\n234\t- **Rep quality:** `1 - 1.6 × mean|r - target|`, shown as Perfect ≥ .85, Good ≥ .65, else Wobbly. Score = mean × 100. Stars 55/75/90.\n235\t- **Fatigue:** tremor on `r`, amplitude `repFatigue × (rep/total)²`, default .10.\n236\t- **The gag:** Coach says \"Last one.\" Then \"Last one.\" Then \"I lied. This is the last one.\" Every exercise runs 2 reps past its announced end.\n237\t- **Set dressing:** a gym wall with the engine's `Mirror` showing your reps (attach in `setup`, stop in `dispose`); giant dumbbells.\n238\t\n239\t### 5.3 Hoop Economics (Beat Beach, aerobics)\n240\t\n241\t**Circle the stick** (or the mouse, or roll W-D-S-A) to keep hoops up.\n242\t\n243\t- Angular speed: from unwrapped `atan2(u)` when `|u| > .5`, smoothed .25 s. Band: `hoopLow` 0.9 to `hoopHigh` 2.4 rev/s, in the current direction.\n244\t- Lift `L`: +.35/s in band, -.25/s out of band, -.5/s wrong way. At 0 the hoop drops, and you pick it up for 1.5 s, with comment.\n245\t- 60 s: anticlockwise 25 s; \"Now the other way.\" clockwise 25 s; either way 10 s.\n246\t- **Thrown hoops:** every ~9 s June winds up on one side, with 1.5 s of telegraph and a landing shadow. Flick `|u.x| > .7` toward that side in the last .4 s to catch it. Up to 4 hoops, each adding +0.5 to the rotation multiplier. Flicking interrupts your circling, which is the trade-off.\n247\t- Score = weighted rotations in band. Stars 35/70/110.\n248\t- **Look:** torus meshes orbiting the hips, tilt by `L`, pelvis circling, sparkles in band.\n249\t\n250\t### 5.4 Step Class (Beat Beach, rhythm)\n251\t\n252\tFootprints scroll to a hit line. Left foot: L1 or L2 / F or ← / left mouse button. Right foot: R1 or R2 / J or → / right mouse button.\n253\t\n254\t- 104 BPM, 32 bars (about 74 s): intro 4, verse 8, chorus 8, breakdown 4, final 8. Charts are strings in data: `L.R.L.R.`.\n255\t- Windows: Perfect ±55 ms, Good ±100, OK ±140 (scale `stepWindow`). Accuracy = (P + .7G + .4O) / N. Stars 55/75/90.\n256\t- **Beat clock:** the song's step clock when audio runs (`ctx.audio.song.start` and `ctx.audio.song.parsed.stepTime`, in the audio context's time; undocumented but present in 0.46.0), otherwise game time (headless, replays). A **Step timing offset** setting covers latency.\n257\t- On pause: stop the music; on resume, count in and restart at the current bar.\n258\t- Avatars step on platforms using the kit's `StepUp` / `StepDown` or your own. Classmates dance with occasional mistakes. Confetti at combo milestones.\n259\t\n260\t### 5.5 Coconut Header (Lagoon Games, balance)\n261\t\n262\tLean left and right along the sand. Dennis runs a catapult.\n263\t\n264\t- Lane ±2 m solo (±0.8 m in lanes). Speed 4.5 m/s × `u.x`.\n265\t- Objects land at head height after 1.4 s with a shadow telegraph, interval 1.3 s → 0.7 s. 70% coconut (head it: +10 × combo tier), 25% flip-flop (dodge it, or -10, combo lost, .6 s daze with circling stars), 5% golden pineapple (+30).\n266\t- Combo tiers ×2/×3/×4 at 5/10/20. 60 s. Stars 150/400/800.\n267\t- Hitstop .05 s on each header. Squash the head. Coconuts fly back at Dennis, who stops being fine.\n268\t\n269\t### 5.6 Slackline (Lagoon Games, balance)\n270\t\n271\tA sagging line across the lagoon between giant dumbbells. Camera low behind you.\n272\t\n273\t- **X is balance, forward is progress.** Push forward (`u.y > .3`) to walk at 1.1 m/s. Walking is less stable, so stopping to recover is a real choice.\n274\t- Defaults ✔ (simulated): effective instability 1.2 standing and 2.6 walking, so `m.inst = 1.2 / T.dotInstability` and `T.slackWalkInst / T.dotInstability`; `m.noise` 1.25; gusts every 6 m, `m.bx` ±1.6 for 1 s, telegraphed 1.5 s by flags and wind lines; fall at `|x| > 1`. Lock y (`dot.y = dot.vy = 0` after each step). Bots walk only while `|x| < .45`.\n275\t- Fall: 1.2 s of slow motion, splash, back to the checkpoint (0/10/20 m). Kevin lands on your head at 18 m and adds a bias until 24 m. 30 m, 75 s limit.\n276\t- Measured ✔: idle never finishes; sloppy rarely; average 38 s with 0.2 falls; good 28 s with 0 falls.\n277\t- **Stars:** ★ finish; ★★ ≤ 1 fall and under 45 s; ★★★ 0 falls and under 32 s. Score `1000 - 60 × falls - 8 × (time - 27)`.\n278\t- Sag the line under your feet (segments updated each frame). Arms out: the kit's `Tiptoe` plus T-pose arms set after the mixer, or a tuned `gaitClip`.\n279\t\n280\t### 5.7 Vitamin Tray (Lagoon Games, balance)\n281\t\n282\tTilt a lacquered tray to roll coloured vitamins into matching cups. Top-down-ish fixed camera.\n283\t\n284\t- **Tilt gravity, not the tray ✔:** the colliders stay flat and static; set `ctx.physics.world.gravity`; a visual `Group` tilts, with the vitamins as its children at `body.translation()`.\n285\t- **The engine's gravity is -24**, not -9.8. Use `trayGravity` 9.8 here and restore it on leaving. Max tilt 14°, driven by `u` with .12 s lag. Enable CCD on the balls.\n286\t- Cups are untagged cylinder triggers ✔ (they see dynamic bodies). 8 vitamins, 2-3 colours, 60 s. +10 correct, -3 wrong; rail gaps in later boards drop a vitamin (-1, respawns after 3 s).\n287\t- Three boards as ASCII in data (`#` wall, `.` floor, `o` start, `R`/`B`/`Y` cups). Stars at 40/60/80% of the board's maximum.\n288\t\n289\t### 5.8 Island Jog (Jogging Path, aerobics)\n290\t\n291\tA rail run around the whole island, the showpiece for the world. `iso` camera following, zoom about 1.4, looking slightly ahead.\n292\t\n293\t- **Alternate left and right** (same inputs as Step) to build cadence (smoothed steps/s). Band 2.4-3.6 steps/s. Same side twice stumbles (-.5 s). Over 3.6 for 2 s gives a Stitch (speed ×.5 for 3 s, remark).\n294\t- Coach paces you on a white mobility scooter at exactly target speed, 3 m/s. Path about 220 m on a Catmull-Rom curve (about 75 s). More than 12 m behind, she waits, unimpressed.\n295\t- Stick X moves you ±1.5 m across the path: collect 18 water cups; dodge sprinklers, picnics, Dennis stretching, and Kevin.\n296\t- Score `70 × pace% + 30 × cups%`. Stars 55/75/90.\n297\t- Run clip: `gaitClip(HERO_RIG, { name: 'Jog', frames: 16, speed: 3, stance: .42, hip: -.04, bob: .03, lift: .1, lean: 6, sway: 2, arms: { swing: 30, elbow: 75 } })` ✔, with `action.timeScale = speed / 3`.\n298\t\n299\t### 5.9 Pier Jump (Lagoon Games, balance)\n300\t\n301\tSide-on (`side` preset), three attempts, three judges with paddles.\n302\t\n303\t1. **Run-up (5 s):** hold the Dot in a tuck zone (`y` -.8 to -.4) to build speed 6→14 m/s.\n304\t2. **Lip:** press ✕ / Space / click as you reach it. Power `p = 1 - |Δt| / .25`.\n305\t3. **Flight (3-4 s):** `m.inst` 3, ring .45; staying in the ring carries you farther.\n306\t4. **Cannonball:** press in the last .5 s for style points. Splash: hitstop .1 s, `timeScale` .25 for .8 s, huge particles, ripples, shake.\n307\t\n308\tDistance = `speed × (.8 + .6p) × (.6 + .4 × ringFrac) × 2.14` m. Stars at 18/28/38 m on the best attempt. Judges react.\n309\t\n310\t### 5.10 The Long Stand (finale, Day 7)\n311\t\n312\tThe plaza at golden hour, everyone watching. Stillness scoring with this data timeline; no way to fail:\n313\t\n314\t| t (s) | Event | Effect |\n315\t|---|---|---|\n316\t| 0 | Coach: \"Stand.\" | ring .40 |\n317\t| 10 | Kevin lands on your head | bias x +.10 |\n318\t| 20 | Dennis sneezes | impulse y -.6 |\n319\t| 30 | | ring .34 |\n320\t| 35 | Light rain (`Precipitation`) | noise ×1.2 |\n321\t| 45 | Marguerite applauds, slowly | Ounce: \"Ignore her.\" |\n322\t| 50 | | ring .28 |\n323\t| 55 | June: \"Are you nearly done?\" | text drifts across the dial |\n324\t| 65 | Kettlebell Volcano rumbles | shake, random impulse |\n325\t| 75 | Rain stops, sun out, music swells | ring .24 |\n326\t| 85 | Kevin leaves | bias off, impulse |\n327\t| 90 | Coach: \"You may blink.\" | end |\n328\t\n329\tThen the confession and ending, played with a `Timeline` (section 13). At 0★: \"You stood. Sort of.\"\n330\t\n331\t---\n332\t\n333\t## 6. Weigh-In and Wobble Age\n334\t\n335\tYour avatar steps onto Ounce (\"Ow.\" every time; the delivery varies). Close-up, huge dial, tiny animated needle.\n336\t\n337\t| Test | Rules (12-15 s) | Result T (0..1) |\n338\t|---|---|---|\n339\t| Stillness | Ring .30. Dot and ring fade after 3 s: \"Eyes closed.\" | in-ring fraction |\n340\t| Lean | Three targets (\"70% left\"). Reach and hold ±.1 for 1.5 s each. | based on time |\n341\t| Chase | Follow a slow target on a Lissajous path | 1 - mean distance × 2 |\n342\t\n343\tThen one question (section 8), then the reveal: the needle spins, the number rolls, Ounce remarks.\n344\t\n345\t```\n346\ttrue  = round(20 + 60 × (1 - mean(last 3 T))) - min(20, totalStars × 0.5), clamped to 18..95\n347\tshown = true + 7 until the confession, then true\n348\t```\n349\t\n350\tThe chart draws 7 points. At the confession every point animates down by 7. Test it: an expert bot ends lower than an average one, an idle bot stays high, and the confession subtracts exactly 7.\n351\t\n352\t---\n353\t\n354\t## 7. The island hub\n355\t\n356\t`iso` camera ✔ (`{ preset: 'iso', zoom: 1.15 }`) with `PlatformerCharacter` and the kit's full moveset. Jumping and waving have no purpose and should still be there. Island about 44 × 34 m. The top of the screen is -x, -z: **put tall things in that quadrant** so they never hide the player.\n357\t\n358\t| Place | Near (x, z) | Purpose |\n359\t|---|---|---|\n360\t| Weigh-In Terrace | (0, 3) | Day start, Ounce's dais |\n361\t| Pavilion of Holding Still | (-11, 3), sky-blue roof | Poses |\n362\t| The Rep Shed | (11, 3), orange roof | Reps |\n363\t| Beat Beach | (12, -10), on sand | Hoop, Step |\n364\t| Lagoon Games | (-12, -10), pier | Header, Slackline, Tray, Pier Jump |\n365\t| Jogging Path gate | (0, 11) | Island Jog |\n366\t| Sweat Jar | (4, -4) | Drips, milestones |\n367\t| Locker Room | (-4, -4) | Avatar |\n368\t| Hall of Numbers | (0, -8) | Records, chart, calendar |\n369\t| Hammock between palms | (14, 10) | End the day |\n370\t| Scenery | | Kettlebell Volcano (-15, -14), Foam Roller Hills (15, -14), Dumbbell Bridge over an inlet, medicine balls, gulls (`Boids`) overhead |\n371\t\n372\t- **Interaction:** a trigger shows `[✕] Enter: Pavilion of Holding Still`. Confirm opens a station menu: activities with stars and best score, locked rows greyed with the unlock day, one remark.\n373\t- **Mouse:** hovering a station highlights it and names it; clicking walks there and enters; clicking ground walks there (pointer ray via `camera.rayAt`, then `castRay`). Steer the hero by writing its `MoveInput.move`.\n374\t- **HUD:** day top-left, Drips top-right, today's prescription under the day, prompts bottom. Nothing else.\n375\t- **Ounce** hops after you (`Squash`, tween) and barks at most every 6 s.\n376\t- **Life:** classmates around the island doing small loops (stretching, sitting, failing).\n377\t\n378\t---\n379\t\n380\t## 8. Writing\n381\t\n382\t**Voice:** short declarative sentences. Polite, clinical, caring, faintly wounding. A remark should sound like it came from a nurse who has read your file. Understatement over exclamation. Numbers delivered gently. Humour comes from what is implied. No sarcasm tags, no memes, no pop-culture references, no swearing. Respect the taste rule in section 1.\n383\t\n384\t**Format:** at most 3 lines of 52 characters. Write at least 120 lines beyond the samples: every activity needs lines for 0/1/2/3★, first time and repeat; plus barks for ring entry and exit, falls and streaks.\n385\t\n386\t**Samples, in voice. Use them, then write more like them.**\n387\t\n388\t*Ounce:*\n389\t- \"Hello! I'm Ounce. Please step on me.\" / \"Ow.\" / \"That was a joke. I can't feel pain. I can only keep records.\"\n390\t- \"How old do you feel? This is not binding.\"\n391\t- \"Your Wobble Age is 68. Is that close? No? I'm so sorry.\"\n392\t- \"Thank you. I have written that down.\"\n393\t- \"You wobbled 14 times. I only counted the ones you noticed.\"\n394\t- \"Your left side is doing very well. I'll let your right side know.\"\n395\t- \"No change since yesterday. Which is, in a way, consistent.\"\n396\t- \"Excellent! ...Compared to Dennis.\"\n397\t- \"I'm not angry. Scales don't get angry. We get accurate.\"\n398\t- \"Have you been stretching? Please don't lie. I'm very sensitive.\"\n399\t- \"Today's concern: your ankles. I've been concerned all night.\"\n400\t- \"Good morning. You look rested. I can't see. But you felt rested.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d022da92-c114-4f47-a8f5-ca68c59e3f52","uuid":"9da65ae8-6266-4893-8812-b0c9698e8c6b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T08:21:47.648Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-012138__x6981e3/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Game plan: an original fitness comedy inspired by Wii Fit\n\nYou are the builder. Build this game from this plan and your brief. Nobody will answer questions, so where the plan leaves something open, decide and move on.\n\n- **The title is yours.** Pick it the way your brief says. It lives in one constant (`src/data/title.js`) and nothing else hard-codes it. In this plan `{TITLE}` stands for it.\n- **Paths are relative to your dev folder.** \"Root\" means its parent, where your brief puts the deliverable html and the exit-interview file. Use the names your brief gives.\n- **✔ means the director ran it** on this machine against engine kit 0.46.0 and hub client 1.4.0, in the form written here. Don't re-derive it. If your download is newer, read its changelog from 0.46.0 on.\n- **Read in this order:** this plan; the kit's `GUIDE.md` and `ASSETS.md`, whole; the hub's `README.md`, whole. Search `API.md` and `LIBRARY.md`; don't read them whole.\n- Your brief's rules on folders, ports, processes, browsers and test reporting still apply. They are repeated here only where they shape the work.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**A week at a spotless wellness island run by a small, polite, deeply judgmental bathroom scale. Your whole body is one dot. Keep it in the circle.**\n\n- **Genre:** fitness and balance minigame collection, inside a light life-sim week. (Catalogue genre: Sports.)\n- **Perspective:** isometric on the island; fixed front-on for class activities; other fixed views per activity (section 5).\n- **Length:** a story week of 7 short days, 16 minutes at the quickest and about an hour for a player who tries everything. Everything is also playable at once from Drop-In Class.\n\n### Pillars (use these to settle any open question)\n\n1. **You are a dot.** Almost everything is one verb: lean. The left stick, WASD or the mouse moves your centre of balance, and it has weight and sway. Poses, reps, hoops, slacklines and the finale are all different ways of moving or holding the Dot.\n2. **Politely judged.** Everything you do gets a calm, clinical, faintly wounding remark. You cannot lose. You can only be noted.\n3. **One small day at a time.** Short sessions, a morning number, a chart, a jar slowly filling.\n4. **Always in class.** Other people are always beside you: classmates alone, family on the couch, friends as ghosts online. Everyone wobbles.\n\n### What it takes from the source, and how it stays original\n\n| Distinctive in the source | Here |\n|---|---|\n| Your body is the controller; a centre-of-balance dot | The Dot: real sway physics on a stick, keys or mouse |\n| A talking device that politely judges you | Ounce, a mint retro dial scale whose dial is its face |\n| A calm instructor demonstrating | Coach Plumb, 81, unblinking, never off vertical |\n| Daily check-in, one number that sums you up, a graph | Weigh-In, Wobble Age, a line chart with a twist |\n| Yoga, strength, aerobics, balance games | Four stations on the island |\n| Minutes banked toward unlocks | Drips in the Sweat Jar |\n| A bright, clean, soft-spoken world | White tile plaza on a pastel island made of giant gym equipment |\n| A household sharing one device | Family Hour on one TV, AI Hub sessions online |\n\n**Do not use** any Nintendo name, character, look, music, level or minigame name: no Wii, Mii, Balance Board, Wii Fit Trainer, Wuhu Island or Fit Age. No white-board host, no sound-alike tunes, no channel-style menus. Mechanics may rhyme; everything you can see, hear or read is new.\n\n**Taste rule:** no jokes about a real person's weight, body shape or eating, and no health claims. Ounce judges wobble, knees, posture and life choices, never bodies. The game asks for no real health data. The About screen says: \"This is not medical advice. Ounce is a scale.\"\n\n---\n\n## 2. World and cast\n\n**Tare Island** (\"Everything here starts at zero.\") is a tiny resort whose landscape is exercise equipment at enormous scale: Kettlebell Volcano (dormant, mostly), Foam Roller Hills, the Dumbbell Bridge, medicine-ball boulders. A white-tiled plaza sits at its heart, ringed by lawn, sand and calm sea.\n\n| Who | What | Voice |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ounce** | The host, a scale. Hops after you on the island. Eyes and a red needle on a round dial: the needle is its eyebrow, and it whips, droops or spins with mood. | High, clipped, polite. \"I'd like to weigh in on that.\" |\n| **Coach Plumb** | 81, silver bun, cyan tracksuit, a plumb bob hanging from one wrist. Demonstrates everything without effort. | Low, slow, deadpan. |\n| **Marguerite** | 74, classmate. Has had everything replaced and tops every leaderboard. Friendly menace. | Warm, unhurried. |\n| **Dennis** | 47, classmate. Here after \"an incident with a deck chair\". Overconfident, always nearly injured. Beatable by Day 3. | Quick, hearty. |\n| **June** | 12, Marguerite's granddaughter. Excellent and bored. | Flat. |\n| **Kevin** | A seagull. Lands on people. Ounce speaks of him as staff. | Squawks. |\n\n**The story in one breath:** you arrive by ferry; Ounce weighs you and gives you an alarming Wobble Age. Through the week the classmates hint that something is off (\"Has anyone ever checked that scale?\"; Coach hangs her plumb line beside Ounce: \"You are leaning.\"; Kevin sits on Ounce and the dial reads 7). On Day 7 you face **The Long Stand**: 90 seconds of standing still while the whole island tries to distract you. Then Ounce confesses: *\"I have been adding seven. To everyone. For years. It felt motivating.\"* The chart of your week visibly slides down seven years. Credits. *\"See you tomorrow. Bring your knees.\"* Play continues as an open-ended Week 2.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Structure of play\n\n### Menu map\n\n```\nTitle (\"Press any button. Gently.\")      idle 10 s on title or any menu -> Attract\n└ Main Menu\n  ├ The Week        pick one of 3 guest slots -> Continue day N / start a week -> Island\n  ├ Drop-In Class   any activity at once, all open, records only, no Drips or story\n  ├ Family Hour     1-4 players on this screen -> Join -> The Medley -> Podium\n  ├ Settings\n  └ About & Credits\n```\n\nIn an AI Hub session none of this appears: the game opens on its own card, then the Medley (section 12).\n\n### Starting a week\n\nName entry, one of 6 preset avatars (the Locker Room changes it later), then the ferry cutscene and Ounce's introduction, which teaches the Dot before the first test: \"This dot is you. Keep it in the circle.\" Then the first Weigh-In. Ounce also asks \"How old do you feel? This is not binding.\" with a number picker; the answer is used once, in the joke when the Wobble Age arrives, and never saved.\n\n### A day in The Week\n\n1. **Morning Weigh-In** (about 45 s, section 6): balance test, one absurd question, today's Wobble Age.\n2. **Free time on the island:** walk between stations, do what you like. Coach's prescription (a daily goal) pays a bonus. Ounce follows and comments.\n3. **Hammock** ends the day once you've done at least one activity. Leaving early earns a guilt-trip, never a block.\n4. **Evening Report** (about 20 s): minutes, Drips, stars, the chart, tomorrow's \"concern\", new unlocks. The light goes to dusk (`applySky(engine, skyAt(19))`), then fade to morning.\n\n### The week\n\n| Day | Test | Coach's prescription (+5 Drips) | Opens today | Story beat |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Stillness | Holding Still and one other | Apologetic Tree, Startled Heron, Squats of Regret, Hoop Economics | Ferry. \"Please step on me.\" \"Ow.\" \"This dot is you. Keep it in the circle. That's it. That's the game. Everything else is decoration.\" |\n| 2 | Lean | 2★ in anything | Coconut Header, Apology Lunges, Reluctant Warrior (adds breathing), Locker Room | Dennis explains the deck chair. Nobody asked. |\n| 3 | Chase | Something you haven't done | Step Class, Slackline, The Chair Nobody Asked For | Marguerite: \"Has anyone ever checked that scale?\" Ounce: \"I'd like to weigh in on that. No.\" |\n| 4 | Stillness | 3 activities | Vitamin Tray, Overhead Mortgage, Tax Season | Coach hangs her plumb line by Ounce. \"You are leaning.\" \"The island is leaning.\" |\n| 5 | Lean | Beat Dennis at anything | Island Jog, Pier Jump, Plank Negotiation | Kevin sits on Ounce. The dial reads 7. Nobody says anything. |\n| 6 | Chase | 3★ anywhere | Hard versions of everything | Ounce rehearses a speech, badly, behind the Sweat Jar. |\n| 7 | Stillness | Stand. | The Long Stand, then the ending | The confession. The chart slides. Credits. |\n\n### Progress\n\n- **Stars:** every activity gives 0-3★ from thresholds in its data.\n- **Drips:** `2 + 3 × stars + floor(playSeconds / 30)`, plus the +5 goal bonus. They fill the **Sweat Jar**.\n- **Jar milestones** at 10, 25, 45, 70, 100, 135 and 175 Drips each unlock a Locker Room item (second headband, towel cape, \"Confidence: no visible effect\", and so on) and a remark.\n- **Records:** per activity, compared with data-table scores for Dennis (low), June (mid-high) and Marguerite (high). A classmate you beat reacts in the Report.\n- **Saves:** `localStorage`, versioned JSON, one key, 3 guest slots, written after every activity and day. Handle missing storage (Node, private mode) with an in-memory fallback.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The core: the Dot\n\nEverything hangs on this feeling good, so it comes first. A stick mapped straight to position would be trivial, because you would just hold still. So the Dot is an unstable weight: you apply force, and it drifts, sways and overshoots.\n\n### The model ✔ (simulated with human-like bots; these are the defaults)\n\n```js\n// src/core/dot.js: pure, no engine imports. Step in fixedUpdate at 60 Hz. p is in -1.2..1.2.\nexport class Dot {\n  x = 0; y = 0; vx = 0; vy = 0; ux = 0; uy = 0; t = 0;\n  constructor(rand) { this.ph = [rand(), rand(), rand(), rand()].map((r) => r * 6.283); }\n  step(dt, inX, inY, T, m = {}) { // T: tunables. m: per-activity modifiers.\n    this.t += dt;\n    const k = Math.min(1, dt / T.dotLag);\n    this.ux += (inX - this.ux) * k; this.uy += (inY - this.uy) * k;\n    const n = T.dotNoise * (m.noise ?? 1), inst = T.dotInstability * (m.inst ?? 1), auth = T.dotAuthority * (m.auth ?? 1);\n    const nx = n * (Math.sin(1.7 * this.t + this.ph[0]) + 0.6 * Math.sin(3.1 * this.t + this.ph[1]));\n    const ny = n * (Math.sin(1.3 * this.t + this.ph[2]) + 0.6 * Math.sin(2.7 * this.t + this.ph[3]));\n    this.vx += (inst * this.x + auth * this.ux - T.dotDamping * this.vx + nx + (m.bx ?? 0)) * dt;\n    this.vy += (inst * this.y + auth * this.uy - T.dotDamping * this.vy + ny + (m.by ?? 0)) * dt;\n    this.x += this.vx * dt; this.y += this.vy * dt;\n    const d = Math.hypot(this.x, this.y);\n    if (d > 1.2) { const s = 1.2 / d; this.x *= s; this.y *= s; this.vx *= 0.3; this.vy *= 0.3; }\n  }\n}\n// Defaults: dotInstability 0.6, dotAuthority 3.0, dotDamping 3.4, dotNoise 0.8, dotLag 0.08\n```\n\nTop speed is about 0.9 units/s, so a lean feels like shifting a heavy marble. Impulses (a sneeze, a gust) add directly to `vx`/`vy`.\n\n### Lean input\n\nAll devices produce one vector `u` in the unit disc, read per lane through a **driver** (section 16).\n\n| Device | `u` |\n|---|---|\n| Stick | `ctx.input.moveAxis()`; set `input.gamepadDeadzone = 0.12` |\n| Keyboard | WASD / arrows, smoothed (attack 0.10 s, release 0.06 s). **Hold Shift for half lean.** |\n| Mouse | Pointer offset from the dial's centre ÷ `mouseLeanRadius` (70 art px), clamped. It is a force, not a target, so you still have to balance. Draw a small crosshair and a line from dial to pointer. |\n| Bot | Delayed PD controller (below) |\n\n### Bots ✔\n\nThe same bots drive classmates, attract mode and the tests:\n\n```\neach step: seen = state tau seconds ago;  d += (rand-0.5)*err*dt*6;  d *= 0.995;\n           u = -kp*(seen.p - target) - kd*seen.v + d;  clamp to the unit disc\nsloppy  tau .42 kp .7  kd .12 err 1.3\naverage tau .30 kp 1.1 kd .35 err .8\ngood    tau .22 kp 1.5 kd .55 err .45\nexpert  tau .17 kp 1.9 kd .7  err .2\n```\n\n**Measured time in ring ✔** (35 s, 40 seeds, first 2 s not counted):\n\n| Ring radius | .40 easy | .30 normal | .22 hard | .16 brutal |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| idle | 11% | 7% | 4% | 2% |\n| sloppy | 21% | 13% | 7% | 4% |\n| average | 71% | 51% | 31% | 18% |\n| good | 100% | 93% | 74% | 49% |\n| expert | 100% | 100% | 98% | 79% |\n| keyboard, average | 88% | 70% | 47% | 28% |\n| mouse, average | 95% | 82% | 59% | 36% |\n\n**Stars for Dot scores: 45 / 70 / 90.** So at ring .30, doing nothing gets no stars, an average player ★, a good one ★★★. Keep this shape if you retune; the test suite guards it (section 18).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Activities\n\n### Shared flow (`src/scenes/activity/base.js`)\n\n`intro` (camera glide, Coach's one line, a card with goal and controls for the current device) → `practice` (first time only: 8 s, unscored, \"Try it.\") → `countdown` → `play` → `outro` → **Results** (stars pop in one by one, score, Drips, a line chosen by activity and stars, classmates' scores) → Retry / Next / Back.\n\n- Each activity is a scene plus a data entry in `src/data/activities.js`: id, name, station, camera, song, tunables, star thresholds, unlock day, `hard` overrides, lines.\n- No fail states. Falls cost points and dignity.\n- **Each activity defines its bot play for a given skill** (circling at a set speed, pressing within a timing error, heading the nearest coconut). The Dot bots in section 4 cover the balance parts. Classmates, attract mode and the tests all use it.\n- Every activity must be readable within 5 seconds with no text.\n- **Hard versions** apply data overrides (smaller rings, faster tempo).\n- **Lanes:** Holding Still, Reps, Hoop, Header and Step run in **lane staging**, up to 4 mats in a row, each with its own driver (local, bot or ghost). Solo play puts you on the centre mat with two classmates as bots.\n\n**Lane camera ✔ (3 mats):** `{ preset: 'fixed', position: [0, 2.3, 8.2], target: [0, 1.15, 0], fov: 30 }`, mats at x = -2.2, 0, 2.2, z = 0.8. **For 4 mats ✔:** position `[0, 3, 9.5]`, target `[0, 1, 0]`, fov 32, mats at x = -3, -1, 1, 3. Multiply the distance by `max(1, (16/9) / aspect)` so narrow windows don't clip the outer mats.\n\n**Staging rules ✔ (found in test renders):**\n- Put Coach to one side of the player, not directly behind; their silhouettes merged.\n- Never draw the dial over an avatar. In 3-mat staging the sky above the player's head is empty: centre the dial at (W/2, 56), radius 44.\n- Make all HUD lines at least 2 art px thick. A 1 px ring vanishes at 10 feet.\n\n**Lean body language:** after `mixer.update(dt)`, add the Dot to the pose: `torso.rotateZ(-dot.x * 0.18); torso.rotateX(-dot.y * 0.12); pelvis.position.x += dot.x * 0.05`. Outside the ring, add a tremble. Far outside, blend to the kit's `Teeter` clip. The player should be able to read the Dot from the body alone.\n\n### 5.1 Holding Still (Pavilion, yoga)\n\nCoach demonstrates on a dais. You hold the pose by keeping the Dot in the ring: settle 4 s (unscored), hold, release 2 s.\n\n| Pose | Clip | Ring | Twist | Hold | Breath |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Apologetic Tree | Tree | .40 | none | 25 s | none |\n| Startled Heron | Heron | .36 | slow bias wander, ±.12 on x | 25 s | none |\n| Reluctant Warrior | Warrior | .32 | y noise ×1.3 | 30 s | 8 s |\n| The Chair Nobody Asked For | Chair | .30 | noise ramps 1→1.5 | 30 s | 8 s |\n| Tax Season | Fold | .28 | bias wander ±.18 | 30 s | 6 s |\n| Plank Negotiation | Plank | .24 | inst ×1.2, tremor after 20 s | 35 s | 6 s |\n\n- **Breath:** a bar rises and falls (\"In…\" / \"Out…\"). Hold R2 / Space / left mouse on In, release on Out. Keep a sync value `s` (0..1, smoothed), and `m.noise *= 1.25 - 0.55 * s`, so breathing well literally calms you.\n- **Score:** `100 × inRing`, or `100 × (0.8 × inRing + 0.2 × breath)` when breath is on. Stars 45/70/90.\n- **Juice:** chime when the Dot enters the ring, ring brightens with time inside, slow camera push-in while steady, classmates wobbling, Kevin on the rail, sunbeams, dust motes.\n\n### 5.2 The Rep Shed (strength)\n\n**You scrub the animation with the stick** ✔ (`action.paused = true; action.time = r * duration; mixer.update(0)`). A marker on a big vertical (or horizontal) track follows the tempo curve; you match it.\n\n| Exercise | Clip | Input | Reps | Tempo |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Squats of Regret | SquatRep | stick down = deeper | 10 | 3.2 s |\n| Apology Lunges | LungeRep, mirrored for left | stick left/right, targets alternate | 10 | 3.2 s |\n| Overhead Mortgage | Press (new) | stick up = press; `repLag` ×1.5 (heavy) | 8 | 3.6 s |\n\n- **Target per rep:** 0→1 in 40% of the tempo, hold 15%, 1→0 in 35%, rest 10%. `r` follows the input with lag `repLag` 0.10 s.\n- **Keyboard uses rate control:** S/W move `r` at the target speed, no key holds it. Otherwise keys could only slam between ends.\n- **Rep quality:** `1 - 1.6 × mean|r - target|`, shown as Perfect ≥ .85, Good ≥ .65, else Wobbly. Score = mean × 100. Stars 55/75/90.\n- **Fatigue:** tremor on `r`, amplitude `repFatigue × (rep/total)²`, default .10.\n- **The gag:** Coach says \"Last one.\" Then \"Last one.\" Then \"I lied. This is the last one.\" Every exercise runs 2 reps past its announced end.\n- **Set dressing:** a gym wall with the engine's `Mirror` showing your reps (attach in `setup`, stop in `dispose`); giant dumbbells.\n\n### 5.3 Hoop Economics (Beat Beach, aerobics)\n\n**Circle the stick** (or the mouse, or roll W-D-S-A) to keep hoops up.\n\n- Angular speed: from unwrapped `atan2(u)` when `|u| > .5`, smoothed .25 s. Band: `hoopLow` 0.9 to `hoopHigh` 2.4 rev/s, in the current direction.\n- Lift `L`: +.35/s in band, -.25/s out of band, -.5/s wrong way. At 0 the hoop drops, and you pick it up for 1.5 s, with comment.\n- 60 s: anticlockwise 25 s; \"Now the other way.\" clockwise 25 s; either way 10 s.\n- **Thrown hoops:** every ~9 s June winds up on one side, with 1.5 s of telegraph and a landing shadow. Flick `|u.x| > .7` toward that side in the last .4 s to catch it. Up to 4 hoops, each adding +0.5 to the rotation multiplier. Flicking interrupts your circling, which is the trade-off.\n- Score = weighted rotations in band. Stars 35/70/110.\n- **Look:** torus meshes orbiting the hips, tilt by `L`, pelvis circling, sparkles in band.\n\n### 5.4 Step Class (Beat Beach, rhythm)\n\nFootprints scroll to a hit line. Left foot: L1 or L2 / F or ← / left mouse button. Right foot: R1 or R2 / J or → / right mouse button.\n\n- 104 BPM, 32 bars (about 74 s): intro 4, verse 8, chorus 8, breakdown 4, final 8. Charts are strings in data: `L.R.L.R.`.\n- Windows: Perfect ±55 ms, Good ±100, OK ±140 (scale `stepWindow`). Accuracy = (P + .7G + .4O) / N. Stars 55/75/90.\n- **Beat clock:** the song's step clock when audio runs (`ctx.audio.song.start` and `ctx.audio.song.parsed.stepTime`, in the audio context's time; undocumented but present in 0.46.0), otherwise game time (headless, replays). A **Step timing offset** setting covers latency.\n- On pause: stop the music; on resume, count in and restart at the current bar.\n- Avatars step on platforms using the kit's `StepUp` / `StepDown` or your own. Classmates dance with occasional mistakes. Confetti at combo milestones.\n\n### 5.5 Coconut Header (Lagoon Games, balance)\n\nLean left and right along the sand. Dennis runs a catapult.\n\n- Lane ±2 m solo (±0.8 m in lanes). Speed 4.5 m/s × `u.x`.\n- Objects land at head height after 1.4 s with a shadow telegraph, interval 1.3 s → 0.7 s. 70% coconut (head it: +10 × combo tier), 25% flip-flop (dodge it, or -10, combo lost, .6 s daze with circling stars), 5% golden pineapple (+30).\n- Combo tiers ×2/×3/×4 at 5/10/20. 60 s. Stars 150/400/800.\n- Hitstop .05 s on each header. Squash the head. Coconuts fly back at Dennis, who stops being fine.\n\n### 5.6 Slackline (Lagoon Games, balance)\n\nA sagging line across the lagoon between giant dumbbells. Camera low behind you.\n\n- **X is balance, forward is progress.** Push forward (`u.y > .3`) to walk at 1.1 m/s. Walking is less stable, so stopping to recover is a real choice.\n- Defaults ✔ (simulated): effective instability 1.2 standing and 2.6 walking, so `m.inst = 1.2 / T.dotInstability` and `T.slackWalkInst / T.dotInstability`; `m.noise` 1.25; gusts every 6 m, `m.bx` ±1.6 for 1 s, telegraphed 1.5 s by flags and wind lines; fall at `|x| > 1`. Lock y (`dot.y = dot.vy = 0` after each step). Bots walk only while `|x| < .45`.\n- Fall: 1.2 s of slow motion, splash, back to the checkpoint (0/10/20 m). Kevin lands on your head at 18 m and adds a bias until 24 m. 30 m, 75 s limit.\n- Measured ✔: idle never finishes; sloppy rarely; average 38 s with 0.2 falls; good 28 s with 0 falls.\n- **Stars:** ★ finish; ★★ ≤ 1 fall and under 45 s; ★★★ 0 falls and under 32 s. Score `1000 - 60 × falls - 8 × (time - 27)`.\n- Sag the line under your feet (segments updated each frame). Arms out: the kit's `Tiptoe` plus T-pose arms set after the mixer, or a tuned `gaitClip`.\n\n### 5.7 Vitamin Tray (Lagoon Games, balance)\n\nTilt a lacquered tray to roll coloured vitamins into matching cups. Top-down-ish fixed camera.\n\n- **Tilt gravity, not the tray ✔:** the colliders stay flat and static; set `ctx.physics.world.gravity`; a visual `Group` tilts, with the vitamins as its children at `body.translation()`.\n- **The engine's gravity is -24**, not -9.8. Use `trayGravity` 9.8 here and restore it on leaving. Max tilt 14°, driven by `u` with .12 s lag. Enable CCD on the balls.\n- Cups are untagged cylinder triggers ✔ (they see dynamic bodies). 8 vitamins, 2-3 colours, 60 s. +10 correct, -3 wrong; rail gaps in later boards drop a vitamin (-1, respawns after 3 s).\n- Three boards as ASCII in data (`#` wall, `.` floor, `o` start, `R`/`B`/`Y` cups). Stars at 40/60/80% of the board's maximum.\n\n### 5.8 Island Jog (Jogging Path, aerobics)\n\nA rail run around the whole island, the showpiece for the world. `iso` camera following, zoom about 1.4, looking slightly ahead.\n\n- **Alternate left and right** (same inputs as Step) to build cadence (smoothed steps/s). Band 2.4-3.6 steps/s. Same side twice stumbles (-.5 s). Over 3.6 for 2 s gives a Stitch (speed ×.5 for 3 s, remark).\n- Coach paces you on a white mobility scooter at exactly target speed, 3 m/s. Path about 220 m on a Catmull-Rom curve (about 75 s). More than 12 m behind, she waits, unimpressed.\n- Stick X moves you ±1.5 m across the path: collect 18 water cups; dodge sprinklers, picnics, Dennis stretching, and Kevin.\n- Score `70 × pace% + 30 × cups%`. Stars 55/75/90.\n- Run clip: `gaitClip(HERO_RIG, { name: 'Jog', frames: 16, speed: 3, stance: .42, hip: -.04, bob: .03, lift: .1, lean: 6, sway: 2, arms: { swing: 30, elbow: 75 } })` ✔, with `action.timeScale = speed / 3`.\n\n### 5.9 Pier Jump (Lagoon Games, balance)\n\nSide-on (`side` preset), three attempts, three judges with paddles.\n\n1. **Run-up (5 s):** hold the Dot in a tuck zone (`y` -.8 to -.4) to build speed 6→14 m/s.\n2. **Lip:** press ✕ / Space / click as you reach it. Power `p = 1 - |Δt| / .25`.\n3. **Flight (3-4 s):** `m.inst` 3, ring .45; staying in the ring carries you farther.\n4. **Cannonball:** press in the last .5 s for style points. Splash: hitstop .1 s, `timeScale` .25 for .8 s, huge particles, ripples, shake.\n\nDistance = `speed × (.8 + .6p) × (.6 + .4 × ringFrac) × 2.14` m. Stars at 18/28/38 m on the best attempt. Judges react.\n\n### 5.10 The Long Stand (finale, Day 7)\n\nThe plaza at golden hour, everyone watching. Stillness scoring with this data timeline; no way to fail:\n\n| t (s) | Event | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | Coach: \"Stand.\" | ring .40 |\n| 10 | Kevin lands on your head | bias x +.10 |\n| 20 | Dennis sneezes | impulse y -.6 |\n| 30 | | ring .34 |\n| 35 | Light rain (`Precipitation`) | noise ×1.2 |\n| 45 | Marguerite applauds, slowly | Ounce: \"Ignore her.\" |\n| 50 | | ring .28 |\n| 55 | June: \"Are you nearly done?\" | text drifts across the dial |\n| 65 | Kettlebell Volcano rumbles | shake, random impulse |\n| 75 | Rain stops, sun out, music swells | ring .24 |\n| 85 | Kevin leaves | bias off, impulse |\n| 90 | Coach: \"You may blink.\" | end |\n\nThen the confession and ending, played with a `Timeline` (section 13). At 0★: \"You stood. Sort of.\"\n\n---\n\n## 6. Weigh-In and Wobble Age\n\nYour avatar steps onto Ounce (\"Ow.\" every time; the delivery varies). Close-up, huge dial, tiny animated needle.\n\n| Test | Rules (12-15 s) | Result T (0..1) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stillness | Ring .30. Dot and ring fade after 3 s: \"Eyes closed.\" | in-ring fraction |\n| Lean | Three targets (\"70% left\"). Reach and hold ±.1 for 1.5 s each. | based on time |\n| Chase | Follow a slow target on a Lissajous path | 1 - mean distance × 2 |\n\nThen one question (section 8), then the reveal: the needle spins, the number rolls, Ounce remarks.\n\n```\ntrue  = round(20 + 60 × (1 - mean(last 3 T))) - min(20, totalStars × 0.5), clamped to 18..95\nshown = true + 7 until the confession, then true\n```\n\nThe chart draws 7 points. At the confession every point animates down by 7. Test it: an expert bot ends lower than an average one, an idle bot stays high, and the confession subtracts exactly 7.\n\n---\n\n## 7. The island hub\n\n`iso` camera ✔ (`{ preset: 'iso', zoom: 1.15 }`) with `PlatformerCharacter` and the kit's full moveset. Jumping and waving have no purpose and should still be there. Island about 44 × 34 m. The top of the screen is -x, -z: **put tall things in that quadrant** so they never hide the player.\n\n| Place | Near (x, z) | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Weigh-In Terrace | (0, 3) | Day start, Ounce's dais |\n| Pavilion of Holding Still | (-11, 3), sky-blue roof | Poses |\n| The Rep Shed | (11, 3), orange roof | Reps |\n| Beat Beach | (12, -10), on sand | Hoop, Step |\n| Lagoon Games | (-12, -10), pier | Header, Slackline, Tray, Pier Jump |\n| Jogging Path gate | (0, 11) | Island Jog |\n| Sweat Jar | (4, -4) | Drips, milestones |\n| Locker Room | (-4, -4) | Avatar |\n| Hall of Numbers | (0, -8) | Records, chart, calendar |\n| Hammock between palms | (14, 10) | End the day |\n| Scenery | | Kettlebell Volcano (-15, -14), Foam Roller Hills (15, -14), Dumbbell Bridge over an inlet, medicine balls, gulls (`Boids`) overhead |\n\n- **Interaction:** a trigger shows `[✕] Enter: Pavilion of Holding Still`. Confirm opens a station menu: activities with stars and best score, locked rows greyed with the unlock day, one remark.\n- **Mouse:** hovering a station highlights it and names it; clicking walks there and enters; clicking ground walks there (pointer ray via `camera.rayAt`, then `castRay`). Steer the hero by writing its `MoveInput.move`.\n- **HUD:** day top-left, Drips top-right, today's prescription under the day, prompts bottom. Nothing else.\n- **Ounce** hops after you (`Squash`, tween) and barks at most every 6 s.\n- **Life:** classmates around the island doing small loops (stretching, sitting, failing).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Writing\n\n**Voice:** short declarative sentences. Polite, clinical, caring, faintly wounding. A remark should sound like it came from a nurse who has read your file. Understatement over exclamation. Numbers delivered gently. Humour comes from what is implied. No sarcasm tags, no memes, no pop-culture references, no swearing. Respect the taste rule in section 1.\n\n**Format:** at most 3 lines of 52 characters. Write at least 120 lines beyond the samples: every activity needs lines for 0/1/2/3★, first time and repeat; plus barks for ring entry and exit, falls and streaks.\n\n**Samples, in voice. Use them, then write more like them.**\n\n*Ounce:*\n- \"Hello! I'm Ounce. Please step on me.\" / \"Ow.\" / \"That was a joke. I can't feel pain. I can only keep records.\"\n- \"How old do you feel? This is not binding.\"\n- \"Your Wobble Age is 68. Is that close? No? I'm so sorry.\"\n- \"Thank you. I have written that down.\"\n- \"You wobbled 14 times. I only counted the ones you noticed.\"\n- \"Your left side is doing very well. I'll let your right side know.\"\n- \"No change since yesterday. Which is, in a way, consistent.\"\n- \"Excellent! ...Compared to Dennis.\"\n- \"I'm not angry. Scales don't get angry. We get accurate.\"\n- \"Have you been stretching? Please don't lie. I'm very sensitive.\"\n- \"Today's concern: your ankles. I've been concerned all night.\"\n- \"Good morning. You look rested. I can't see. But you felt rested.\"","numLines":400,"startLine":1,"totalLines":993}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FRvGYj4iK4UAvttBpud8Sx","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
